const DOCS_DATA = {
  "pt": {
    "getting-started": {
      "id": "getting-started",
      "title": "Guia de Início Rápido & Primeiros Passos",
      "category": "Começando",
      "summary": "Aprenda os fundamentos do ChaosSQL, como instalar a ferramenta com zero dependências externas (Pure Go, CGO_ENABLED=0) e executar seu primeiro teste de concorrência com detecção de anomalia e redução causal em menos de 5 minutos.",
      "content": "<h2>O Que é o ChaosSQL?</h2>\n<p>O <strong>ChaosSQL</strong> é um Fuzzer Determinístico de Concorrência e Invariantes para Bancos de Dados SQL, criado por Ricardo Bregalda (Studio Bregalda). Em sistemas concorrentes e microsserviços modernos, bugs de concorrência — como <em>Lost Updates</em>, <em>Write Skew</em>, <em>Phantom Reads</em> e <em>Deadlocks</em> transitórios — são extremamente difíceis de reproduzir e depurar. Testes de unidade convencionais são sequenciais e determinísticos por natureza, enquanto testes de carga estocásticos (como JMeter ou k6) geram ruído desmedido e raramente reproduzem as trocas de contexto (<em>context switches</em>) críticas necessárias para disparar a anomalia.</p>\n\n<p>O ChaosSQL aborda essa lacuna fundamental unindo a engenharia do caos com três pilares da computação teórica:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Agendamento Probabilístico Estocástico (PCT-SQL):</strong> Baseado no algoritmo <em>Probabilistic Concurrency Testing</em> de Burckhardt &amp; Musuvathi (ASPLOS 2010), fornecendo garantias matemáticas formais de probabilidade mínima de detecção em espaços combinatórios exponenciais.</li>\n  <li><strong>Classificação Automática de Anomalias via Grafos de Adya:</strong> Reconstrói o Grafo de Serialização Direta (<em>Direct Serialization Graph</em>) com base na tese seminal de Atul Adya (MIT 1999) e nas observações de caixa-preta do Elle (VLDB 2020), identificando arestas de conflito $rw$ (anti-dependência), $ww$ (sobreposição) e $wr$ (dependência de leitura).</li>\n  <li><strong>Causal Delta-Debugging ($ddmin$):</strong> Implementa o algoritmo de Andreas Zeller adaptado com preservação de integridade referencial, reduzindo centenas de transações caóticas a um caso de teste <em>1-minimal</em> de apenas 2 operações em milissegundos.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"docs-callout info\">\n  <strong>Zero CGO &amp; Pure Go:</strong> O ChaosSQL compila para um único binário executável estático sem necessidade de ferramentas de compilação em C (<code>CGO_ENABLED=0</code>). O motor SQLite embutido roda via transpilador nativo em Go puro (<code>modernc.org/sqlite</code>), permitindo execução instantânea em qualquer container Alpine, WSL2, Linux, macOS ou Windows.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Requisitos de Sistema &amp; Instalação</h2>\n<p>O ChaosSQL requer apenas o ambiente Go 1.22 ou superior (caso deseje compilar a partir do código-fonte) ou pode ser baixado diretamente como binário standalone.</p>\n\n<h3>Método 1: Instalação Rápida via Go Toolchain</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <button class=\"copy-code-btn\" onclick=\"navigator.clipboard.writeText('go install github.com/bregaldahq/chaossql/cmd/chaossql@latest')\">Copiar</button>\n  <pre><code>go install github.com/bregaldahq/chaossql/cmd/chaossql@latest</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Método 2: Compilação Estática a Partir do Repositório Git</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <button class=\"copy-code-btn\" onclick=\"navigator.clipboard.writeText('git clone https://github.com/bregaldahq/chaossql.git\\ncd chaossql\\nCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags=\\\"-s -w\\\" -o bin/chaossql ./cmd/chaossql')\">Copiar</button>\n  <pre><code># 1. Clonar o repositório oficial\ngit clone https://github.com/bregaldahq/chaossql.git\ncd chaossql\n\n# 2. Compilar binário estático de alta performance\nCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o bin/chaossql ./cmd/chaossql\n\n# 3. Testar a instalação\n./bin/chaossql --help</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Método 3: Execução via Docker Container</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>docker run --rm -v $(pwd):/workspace -w /workspace ghcr.io/bregaldahq/chaossql:latest run specs/banking.yaml</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Seu Primeiro Teste em 5 Minutos (Passo a Passo)</h2>\n<p>Vamos criar e testar um cenário clássico de perda de atualização bancária (<em>Lost Update</em>, anomalia $P4$), onde dois clientes sacam dinheiro simultaneamente da mesma conta sob isolamento permissivo.</p>\n\n<h3>Passo 1: Criar o Scaffolding com <code>chaossql init</code></h3>\n<p>O comando <code>init</code> cria a estrutura de diretórios e arquivos de modelo prontos para execução:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql init quickstart_banking --driver sqlite</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>A estrutura de arquivos criada é:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>schema.sql</code>: Esquema das tabelas e índices DDL.</li>\n  <li><code>seed.sql</code>: Carga inicial de dados DML.</li>\n  <li><code>chaos.yaml</code>: Especificação do motor, workers, invariantes e operações.</li>\n  <li><code>README.md</code>: Documentação contextual do cenário.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Passo 2: Definir o Esquema DDL (<code>schema.sql</code>)</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- Tabela de contas bancárias\nCREATE TABLE accounts (\n    id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    owner TEXT NOT NULL,\n    balance INT NOT NULL\n);</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Passo 3: Inserir a Carga Inicial (<code>seed.sql</code>)</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- Saldo inicial de R$ 1.000 para Alice\nINSERT INTO accounts (id, owner, balance) VALUES (1, 'Alice', 1000);</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Passo 4: Configurar a Especificação (<code>chaos.yaml</code>)</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">version: \"1.0\"\nname: \"quickstart_banking\"\ndescription: \"Verificação de integridade de saldo sob saques simultâneos\"\n\ndatabase:\n  driver: \"sqlite\"\n  schema: \"schema.sql\"\n  seed: \"seed.sql\"\n\nengine:\n  workers: 4          # 4 goroutines concorrentes\n  iterations: 20      # 20 operações de saque no total\n  seed: 42            # Semente determinística reproduzível\n  jitter_ms: [1, 5]   # Micro-atrasos estocásticos de 1ms a 5ms\n\ninvariants:\n  - name: \"total_balance_consistency\"\n    query: \"SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1;\"\n    assert: \"balance == 1000 - (total_completed * 100)\"\n\noperations:\n  - name: \"withdraw_100\"\n    steps:\n      - \"SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1 -> cur\"\n      - \"UPDATE accounts SET balance = {cur - 100} WHERE id = 1\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Passo 5: Executar o Fuzzer com <code>chaossql run</code></h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql run quickstart_banking/chaos.yaml --seed=42 --workers=4</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Passo 6: Analisando o Diagnóstico no Terminal</h3>\n<p>O ChaosSQL executa os 4 workers simultaneamente, intercalando as operações de leitura (<code>SELECT</code>) e escrita (<code>UPDATE</code>) com jitter estocástico. Em poucos milissegundos, o fuzzer detecta a violação da invariante:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Violação Identificada:</strong> <code>balance == 1000 - (total_completed * 100)</code> falhou (esperado: 0, real: 500).</li>\n  <li><strong>Classificação Adya:</strong> Anomalia <code>P4_LOST_UPDATE</code> ($G\\text{-single}$ cycle).</li>\n  <li><strong>Grafo de Conflito:</strong> <code>T1 ──(rw)──► T2 ──(ww)──► T1</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Causal Delta-Debugging ($ddmin$):</strong> Reduziu o trace original de 20 operações para apenas 2 operações críticas em 68ms (90.0% de redução).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"docs-callout tip\">\n  <strong>Interface Visual Interativa:</strong> Adicione a flag <code>--ui</code> ao executar qualquer teste (ex: <code>chaossql run chaos.yaml --ui</code>) para inicializar instantaneamente o servidor de visualização local em <code>http://127.0.0.1:8090</code>, com linha do tempo Gantt e visualização de grafos SVG.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Demonstrações Flagship Embutidas</h2>\n<p>O ChaosSQL inclui 10 cenários pré-configurados prontos para execução imediata via comando <code>chaossql demo</code>:</p>\n\n<table class=\"matrix-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Comando</th>\n      <th>Fenômeno</th>\n      <th>Código Adya</th>\n      <th>Severidade</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo banking</code></td>\n      <td>Banking Lost Update</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">P4</span></td>\n      <td>Alta (Perda de Mutação)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo inventory</code></td>\n      <td>Inventory Oversell</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">A3</span></td>\n      <td>Crítica (Estoque Negativo)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo hospital</code></td>\n      <td>Hospital Write Skew</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">A5B</span></td>\n      <td>Crítica (Violação Global)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo financial</code></td>\n      <td>Financial Read Skew</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">A5A</span></td>\n      <td>Média (Auditoria Quebrada)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo auction</code></td>\n      <td>Auction Dirty Write</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">G0</span></td>\n      <td>Crítica (Violação 2PL)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo crypto</code></td>\n      <td>Crypto Arbitrage Info Cycle</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">G1c</span></td>\n      <td>Alta (Fluxo Circular)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo flash_crash</code></td>\n      <td>Flash Crash Dirty Read</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">G1a</span></td>\n      <td>Crítica (Dados Abortados)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo ticket</code></td>\n      <td>Ticket Anti-Dependency</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">G2</span></td>\n      <td>Alta (Reserva Duplicada)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo deadlock</code></td>\n      <td>Deadlock Cycle &amp; Recovery</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">G-DL</span></td>\n      <td>Média (Bloqueio Mútuo)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo fk</code></td>\n      <td>Foreign Key Cascade Deadlock</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">G-DL</span></td>\n      <td>Alta (Conflito Cascata)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>"
    },
    "dsl-spec": {
      "id": "dsl-spec",
      "title": "Especificação da Linguagem Declarativa (chaos.yaml DSL)",
      "category": "Especificação",
      "summary": "Referência exaustiva da DSL declarativa YAML: taxonomia completa de campos, sintaxe de captura léxica, interpolação de expressões dinâmicas, predicados de guarda condicional, asserções de invariantes e injeção de falhas controladas.",
      "content": "<h2>Visão Geral da Linguagem Declarativa (DSL)</h2>\n<p>O ChaosSQL utiliza uma DSL declarativa em YAML (<code>chaos.yaml</code>) para especificar cenários de teste de concorrência de forma desacoplada do código da aplicação. A especificação modela formalmente o ambiente de banco de dados, o agendamento de workers, as transações concorrentes e as invariantes indutivas que garantem a consistência dos dados.</p>\n\n<h2>Taxonomia Completa de Propriedades Raiz</h2>\n<table class=\"matrix-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Campo</th>\n      <th>Tipo</th>\n      <th>Obrigatório</th>\n      <th>Descrição Técnica</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>version</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Sim</td>\n      <td>Versão da especificação da DSL (suporta <code>\"1.0\"</code> e <code>\"1.1\"</code>).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>name</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Sim</td>\n      <td>Identificador único do cenário (ex: <code>banking_lost_update</code>).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>description</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Não</td>\n      <td>Documentação textual da hipótese concorrente e regras de negócio.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>database</code></td>\n      <td><code>object</code></td>\n      <td>Sim</td>\n      <td>Configuração do driver, scripts DDL de esquema e DML de seed.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>engine</code></td>\n      <td><code>object</code></td>\n      <td>Sim</td>\n      <td>Parâmetros do motor de execução: workers, semente PRNG e jitter.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>operations</code></td>\n      <td><code>array</code></td>\n      <td>Sim</td>\n      <td>Lista de transações atômicas a serem executadas pelos workers.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>invariants</code></td>\n      <td><code>array</code></td>\n      <td>Sim</td>\n      <td>Asserções de integridade global pós-execução e contínuas.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>faults</code></td>\n      <td><code>object</code></td>\n      <td>Não</td>\n      <td>Configurações de injeção de falhas estocásticas (aborts, desconexões).</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>O Bloco <code>database</code></h2>\n<p>Define o motor de banco de dados alvo e os scripts de inicialização de estado:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">database:\n  driver: \"sqlite\"             # Suportados: \"sqlite\", \"postgres\", \"mysql\"\n  schema: \"schema.sql\"         # Caminho relativo para arquivo DDL ou string SQL inline\n  seed: \"seed.sql\"             # Caminho relativo para arquivo DML de dados iniciais\n  dsn: \"file:memdb?mode=memory&cache=shared\" # DSN opcional (obrigatório para Postgres/MySQL)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>O Bloco <code>engine</code></h2>\n<p>Controla a orquestração concorrente do agendamento PCT-SQL:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">engine:\n  workers: 8                   # Quantidade de goroutines simultâneas\n  iterations: 50               # Total de transações a serem agendadas\n  seed: 1337                   # Semente do PRNG PCG64 (determinismo estrito)\n  jitter_ms: [1, 10]           # Faixa de atraso estocástico [min, max] entre passos SQL\n  isolation: \"READ COMMITTED\"  # Opcional: forçar nível transacional</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Operações, Captura de Variáveis e Expressões Dinâmicas</h2>\n<p>Cada item em <code>operations</code> define uma transação concorrente atômica. Cada passo (<em>step</em>) é executado sequencialmente dentro da transação alocada para um worker.</p>\n\n<h3>Sintaxe de Captura Léxica (<code>-&gt; var_name</code> ou <code>=&gt; var_name</code>)</h3>\n<p>Permite ler valores de colunas de uma query <code>SELECT</code> e armazená-los no contexto léxico local da transação para uso em instruções subsequentes:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">operations:\n  - name: \"transfer_funds\"\n    steps:\n      # Captura a coluna 'balance' da linha retornada na variável 'current_bal'\n      - \"SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1 -> current_bal\"\n      - \"UPDATE accounts SET balance = {current_bal - 100} WHERE id = 1\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Interpolação de Expressões Dinâmicas (<code>{expr}</code>)</h3>\n<p>O ChaosSQL incorpora o motor de compilação <code>expr-lang</code>, permitindo operações aritméticas, lógicas e chamadas em tempo de execução:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Operações Aritméticas:</strong> <code>{current_bal - 100}</code>, <code>{price * quantity * 0.9}</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Operações Condicionais:</strong> <code>{avail &gt; 0 ? avail - 1 : 0}</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Interpolação de Variáveis:</strong> <code>UPDATE products SET stock = {avail - 1} WHERE id = {target_product}</code></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Predicados de Guarda Condicional em SQL</h3>\n<p>Você pode combinar a sintaxe de expressão com cláusulas <code>WHERE</code> para criar predicados de concorrência com guarda atômica:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">operations:\n  - name: \"conditional_checkout\"\n    steps:\n      - \"SELECT stock FROM products WHERE id = 1 -> avail\"\n      - \"UPDATE products SET stock = {avail - 1} WHERE id = 1 AND {avail > 0}\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Parâmetros Dinâmicos (<code>params</code>)</h3>\n<p>A versão 1.1 da DSL permite declarar variáveis pseudoaleatórias por transação:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">operations:\n  - name: \"dynamic_transfer\"\n    params:\n      sender_id: \"rand_int(1, 10)\"\n      receiver_id: \"rand_int(11, 20)\"\n      amount: \"rand_int(50, 200)\"\n    steps:\n      - \"UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - {amount} WHERE id = {sender_id}\"\n      - \"UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + {amount} WHERE id = {receiver_id}\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Asserções de Invariantes Globais (<code>invariants</code>)</h2>\n<p>As invariantes representam as leis de conservação do sistema que nunca podem ser violadas, independentemente da ordem de intercalação dos workers.</p>\n<table class=\"matrix-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Campo</th>\n      <th>Tipo</th>\n      <th>Descrição</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>name</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Nome descritivo da invariante (ex: <code>conservation_of_money</code>).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>query</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Query SQL agregada executada ao final do teste para obter o estado consolidado.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>assert</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Expressão booleana avaliada sobre o resultado da query.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Variáveis Especiais Injetadas no Contexto da Asserção</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>col_name</code>: O valor retornado por qualquer coluna da query da invariante (ex: <code>total_balance</code>, <code>active_doctors</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>total_completed</code>: Quantidade total de transações confirmadas com sucesso (<code>EventCommit</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>total_aborted</code>: Quantidade de transações que abortaram por conflito de serialização ou deadlock.</li>\n  <li><code>total_operations</code>: Total absoluto de operações agendadas.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">invariants:\n  # 1. Lei de conservação de patrimônio\n  - name: \"conservation_of_money\"\n    query: \"SELECT sum(balance) AS total FROM accounts;\"\n    assert: \"total == 2000\"\n\n  # 2. Relação linear entre operações concluídas e saldo\n  - name: \"balance_audit\"\n    query: \"SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1;\"\n    assert: \"balance == 1000 - (total_completed * 100)\"\n\n  # 3. Predicado de não-negatividade\n  - name: \"stock_non_negative\"\n    query: \"SELECT stock FROM products WHERE id = 1;\"\n    assert: \"stock >= 0\"\n\n  # 4. Quórum de segurança hospitalar\n  - name: \"at_least_one_doctor_on_call\"\n    query: \"SELECT count(*) AS on_duty FROM doctors WHERE on_call = true;\"\n    assert: \"on_duty >= 1\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Injeção de Falhas Controladas (<code>faults</code>)</h2>\n<p>Permite injetar adversidades de rede e comportamento transacional estocástico:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">faults:\n  transient_abort_rate: 0.15  # 15% de chance de ROLLBACK abrupto no meio de uma transação (testa G1a)\n  connection_drop_rate: 0.05  # 5% de chance de corte de conexão durante a execução\n  latency_spikes:\n    probability: 0.10\n    delay_ms: 100             # Injeta 100ms de atraso assíncrono para ampliar janela de corrida</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"docs-callout warning\">\n  <strong>Linter Estático Obrigatório:</strong> Antes de executar cenários complexos, utilize sempre o comando <code>chaossql validate &lt;arquivo.yaml&gt;</code> para compilar antecipadamente as expressões da DSL e prevenir falhas de tipagem durante a execução.\n</div>"
    },
    "cli-reference": {
      "id": "cli-reference",
      "title": "Manual Completo da CLI & Todas as 12 Flags",
      "category": "Interface",
      "summary": "Guia definitivo de uso da interface de linha de comando do ChaosSQL: documentação exaustiva de todos os 9 subcomandos, parâmetros posicionais, códigos de saída e a tabela completa das 12 flags de execução e exportação.",
      "content": "<h2>Arquitetura e Filosofia da CLI</h2>\n<p>A interface de linha de comando do ChaosSQL foi projetada para engenheiros de sistemas que exigem feedback rápido, controle granular e integração transparente com scripts de automação e pipelines de CI/CD. Baseada na biblioteca Cobra e formatada pelo framework Lipgloss, a CLI oferece saída rica em terminal ANSI, cores tonais suaves do Design System do Studio Bregalda e geração de relatórios padronizados (SARIF, JUnit, HTML, OTEL, Mermaid).</p>\n\n<h2>Tabela Mestra das 12 Flags de Execução e Exportação</h2>\n<p>Todas as flags podem ser passadas nos subcomandos <code>run</code> e <code>demo</code>, sobrescrevendo os valores correspondentes definidos no arquivo de especificação:</p>\n\n<table class=\"matrix-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Flag</th>\n      <th>Tipo</th>\n      <th>Padrão</th>\n      <th>Subcomandos</th>\n      <th>Descrição Técnica</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--seed</code></td>\n      <td><code>uint64</code></td>\n      <td><code>0</code> (lê spec)</td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code>, <code>diff</code></td>\n      <td>Semente estocástica do PRNG PCG64. Garante reprodução determinística bit-a-bit idêntica de qualquer anomalia.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--workers</code></td>\n      <td><code>int</code></td>\n      <td><code>0</code> (lê spec)</td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code>, <code>bench</code></td>\n      <td>Número de goroutines concorrentes executando transações simultâneas contra o banco.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--iterations</code></td>\n      <td><code>int</code></td>\n      <td><code>0</code> (lê spec)</td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Número total de transações/operações a serem agendadas na bateria de testes.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--export-sarif</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"\"</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Caminho de arquivo para exportar os achados no padrão OASIS SARIF v2.1.0 para o GitHub Code Scanning.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--export-html</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"\"</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Gera um relatório HTML interativo independente (~60KB) com swimlane Gantt e visualizador de grafos SVG.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--export-otel</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"\"</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Exporta o trace de execução como OpenTelemetry OTLP JSON para observabilidade no Jaeger ou Datadog.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--export-junit</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"\"</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Exporta os resultados em formato JUnit XML padrão para exibição em painéis de teste de CI/CD.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--export-summary</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"\"</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Exporta relatório formatado em Markdown compatível com <code>$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY</code>.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--export-mermaid</code></td>\n      <td><code>bool</code></td>\n      <td><code>false</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Exporta o diagrama de sequência temporal das transações conflitantes em formato Mermaid (<code>trace.mermaid</code>).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--export-repro</code></td>\n      <td><code>bool</code></td>\n      <td><code>false</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Sintetiza automaticamente um teste standalone em Go (<code>repro_test.go</code>) com a redução 1-minimal do $ddmin$.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--ui</code></td>\n      <td><code>bool</code></td>\n      <td><code>false</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Inicia automaticamente o servidor web visualizador de traces em <code>127.0.0.1:8090</code> e abre o navegador.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--json</code></td>\n      <td><code>bool</code></td>\n      <td><code>false</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code>, <code>bench</code>, <code>diff</code>, <code>matrix</code></td>\n      <td>Emite o resultado estruturado em JSON no stdout para integração com scripts externos.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Referência Exaustiva dos 9 Subcomandos</h2>\n\n<h3>1. <code>chaossql run &lt;chaos.yaml&gt;</code></h3>\n<p>Executa a bateria de testes de concorrência contra o arquivo de especificação informado:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code># Execução padrão\nchaossql run specs/banking_lost_update.yaml\n\n# Execução com override de semente, workers e geração completa de relatórios\nchaossql run specs/banking_lost_update.yaml \\\n  --seed=42 \\\n  --workers=8 \\\n  --iterations=50 \\\n  --export-sarif=reports/audit.sarif \\\n  --export-html=reports/report.html \\\n  --export-junit=reports/junit.xml \\\n  --export-repro \\\n  --ui</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>2. <code>chaossql demo [cenário]</code></h3>\n<p>Executa uma das 10 demonstrações embutidas de anomalias de concorrência com terminal visual e delta-debugging:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql demo banking       # P4 Lost Update\nchaossql demo inventory     # A3 Inventory Oversell\nchaossql demo hospital      # A5B Hospital Write Skew\nchaossql demo financial     # A5A Financial Read Skew\nchaossql demo auction       # G0 Auction Dirty Write\nchaossql demo crypto        # G1c Circular Information Flow\nchaossql demo flash_crash   # G1a Flash Crash Dirty Read\nchaossql demo ticket        # G2 Anti-Dependency Cycle\nchaossql demo deadlock      # G-DL Mutual Lock Deadlock\nchaossql demo fk            # Foreign Key Cascade Deadlock</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>3. <code>chaossql ui &lt;trace.json&gt;</code></h3>\n<p>Inicializa o servidor HTTP local para inspecionar um trace gravado previamente:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql ui trace_dump.json --port=8090 --no-open</code></pre>\n</div>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>--port int</code>: Porta do servidor HTTP local (padrão <code>8090</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>--no-open bool</code>: Não abre automaticamente o navegador padrão do sistema.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. <code>chaossql diff &lt;spec.yaml&gt;</code></h3>\n<p>Executa fuzzing diferencial cruzado comparando o comportamento de dois motores distintos com o mesmo workload e semente:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql diff specs/lost_update.yaml \\\n  --driver-a=sqlite --dsn-a=\":memory:\" \\\n  --driver-b=postgres --dsn-b=\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/testdb\" \\\n  --seed=42</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>5. <code>chaossql replay &lt;result.json&gt;</code></h3>\n<p>Exibe o swimlane cronológico de eventos com marcações de microssegundos no terminal:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql replay result.json --max-events=100</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>6. <code>chaossql bench</code></h3>\n<p>Executa microbenchmarks de vazão do PRNG determinístico e do detector de ciclos Adya:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql bench --duration=3s --workers=8 --json</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>O benchmark atinge tipicamente mais de <strong>13.9 milhões de operações/segundo</strong> em processadores modernos.</p>\n\n<h3>7. <code>chaossql validate &lt;chaos.yaml&gt;</code></h3>\n<p>Linter estático que valida o esquema YAML, compila expressões dinâmicas de invariantes e verifica integridade referencial dos scripts SQL antes da execução:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql validate specs/hospital_write_skew.yaml</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>8. <code>chaossql init &lt;caminho&gt;</code></h3>\n<p>Gera o scaffolding inicial de um novo cenário com arquivos pré-configurados:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql init novo_cenario --driver=postgres --name=auditoria_saldo --force</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>9. <code>chaossql matrix</code></h3>\n<p>Gera uma matriz empírica completa baseada na taxonomia Hermitage (Martin Kleppmann), testando o banco alvo contra todas as 9 anomalias formais:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql matrix --driver=postgres --dsn=\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/testdb\" --markdown</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Códigos de Retorno do Processo (Exit Codes)</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>0</code>: <strong>Sucesso / Safe</strong>. Todas as transações foram executadas e nenhuma invariante foi violada.</li>\n  <li><code>1</code>: <strong>Violação Detectada / Anomaly Found</strong>. Pelo menos uma invariante falhou ou uma anomalia foi classificada (quebra builds de CI propositalmente).</li>\n  <li><code>2</code>: <strong>Erro de Configuração / Syntax Error</strong>. Falha de sintaxe YAML, arquivo ausente ou falha de conexão inicial.</li>\n</ul>"
    },
    "trace-visualizer": {
      "id": "trace-visualizer",
      "title": "Visualizador Interativo de Traces (chaossql ui)",
      "category": "Visualização",
      "summary": "Arquitetura e recursos do visualizador web interativo embutido: navegação pelo Gantt swimlane em microssegundos, inspeção visual do grafo de conflitos de Adya (arestas rw, ww, wr), comparação de delta-debugging e inspetor de queries SQL.",
      "content": "<h2>Arquitetura do Visualizador Web Local (<code>chaossql ui</code>)</h2>\n<p>O Visualizador Interativo de Traces do ChaosSQL é uma aplicação web autônoma e editorial servida diretamente pelo servidor HTTP nativo em Go (<code>net/http</code>). Projetado sob o Design System oficial do Studio Bregalda, ele adota uma paleta tonal cinematográfica e relaxante (Deep Ink <code>#120E1F</code>, Bregalda Purple <code>#4B2E83</code>, Warm Cream <code>#FCFBF8</code>, Signal Yellow <code>#F5C400</code> e Ship Green <code>#22C55E</code>) com tipografia monoespaçada de alta legibilidade (JetBrains Mono) e zero dependências externas (sem CDN, sem Node.js e sem trackers).</p>\n\n<h2>Como Iniciar o Visualizador</h2>\n<h3>Opção 1: Visualização Imediata Pós-Execução</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql run specs/banking_lost_update.yaml --ui</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>Ao término da bateria de testes, o ChaosSQL inicia o servidor local e abre automaticamente o navegador do sistema operacional (usando <code>wslview</code> no WSL2, <code>open</code> no macOS, ou <code>rundll32</code> no Windows).</p>\n\n<h3>Opção 2: Inspecionar Trace Salvo em Disco</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql ui trace_banking_run_42.json --port=8090</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Os 4 Módulos Visuais de Inspeção</h2>\n\n<h3>1. Concurrency Gantt Swimlane (Linha do Tempo em Microssegundos)</h3>\n<p>O Gantt Swimlane mapeia cada worker goroutine ($W_0, W_1, W_2, \\dots$) em sua própria raia temporal horizontal independente:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Eixo Temporal Monotônico:</strong> Marcações de tempo expressas em microssegundos ($\\mu\\text{s}$) desde o início da execução, permitindo identificar com precisão a sobreposição de transações.</li>\n  <li><strong>Blocos de Operações Codificados por Cor:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><span style=\"color: #6366f1;\">● Roxo / Azul:</span> <code>BEGIN</code> e abertura de snapshot da transação.</li>\n      <li><span style=\"color: #94a3b8;\">● Cinza:</span> <code>SELECT</code> e leituras intermediárias.</li>\n      <li><span style=\"color: #f59e0b;\">● Laranja:</span> <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>INSERT</code> e mutações de escrita.</li>\n      <li><span style=\"color: #22c55e;\">● Verde:</span> <code>COMMIT</code> finalizado com sucesso.</li>\n      <li><span style=\"color: #ef4444;\">● Vermelho:</span> <code>ROLLBACK</code> ou falha de concorrência.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Diagnóstico Visual de Janela Crítica:</strong> Revela o momento exato em que o Worker 2 executou sua leitura antes que o Worker 1 realizasse o commit, evidenciando visualmente a leitura suja ou a perda de mutação.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Grafo Direcionado de Dependências de Adya (SVG Interativo)</h3>\n<p>O visualizador renderiza o Grafo de Serialização Direta ($SG(S)$) com nós representando transações concorrentes e arestas direcionadas representando conflitos de dados:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Aresta $rw$ (Anti-Dependency / Anti-Leitura):</strong> Transação $T_1$ leu uma versão antiga de um dado antes de $T_2$ sobrescrevê-lo com uma versão mais recente.</li>\n  <li><strong>Aresta $ww$ (Overwrite / Sobrescrita):</strong> Transação $T_1$ escreveu uma versão de registro que foi posteriormente sobrescrita por $T_2$.</li>\n  <li><strong>Aresta $wr$ (Read Dependency / Dependência de Leitura):</strong> Transação $T_2$ leu o valor gerado e comitado por $T_1$.</li>\n  <li><strong>Destaque de Ciclo Anômalo:</strong> Arestas que formam ciclos direcionados ($T_1 \\xrightarrow{rw} T_2 \\xrightarrow{ww} T_1$) são destacadas em Ouro Bregalda (<code>#F5C400</code>) pulsante, demonstrando a prova matemática da quebra de serializabilidade (Teorema CSR).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Painel de Causal Delta-Debugging ($ddmin$) — Antes vs Depois</h3>\n<p>O módulo shrinker do ChaosSQL reduz o ruído de execuções longas para uma reprodução mínima. O cartão de $ddmin$ exibe:</p>\n<table class=\"matrix-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Métrica</th>\n      <th>Valor Típico</th>\n      <th>Significado</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Trace Original</td>\n      <td>20 a 100 operações</td>\n      <td>Histórico completo executado pelos workers durante o estresse caótico.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Trace Mínimo</td>\n      <td>2 operações</td>\n      <td>Subconjunto <em>1-minimal</em> irredutível que reproduz exatamente a mesma anomalia.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Taxa de Compressão</td>\n      <td>&gt; 90.0%</td>\n      <td>Volume de ruído e transações irrelevantes eliminadas pelo algoritmo.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Tempo de Convergência</td>\n      <td>&lt; 100ms</td>\n      <td>Duração da busca binária causal para encontrar o contraexemplo mínimo.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>4. Inspetor Tabular de Queries &amp; Parâmetros Resolvidos</h3>\n<p>Uma tabela interativa detalhada que lista cada instrução executada no banco de dados na ordem cronológica real:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Resolução de Parâmetros:</strong> Instruções SQL são exibidas com suas variáveis de interpolação resolvidas (ex: <code>UPDATE accounts SET balance = 900 WHERE id = 1</code> em vez de expressões dinâmicas brutas).</li>\n  <li><strong>Duração Individual:</strong> Medição do tempo de execução de cada comando no driver do banco em microssegundos.</li>\n  <li><strong>Afinidade de Goroutine:</strong> Identificação do worker responsável pela query e status de sucesso ou erro retornado pelo motor SQL.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Exportação de Relatórios HTML Standalone</h2>\n<p>Você pode gerar relatórios estáticos autocontidos para anexar a Pull Requests ou compartilhar em discussões de arquitetura:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql run specs/banking_lost_update.yaml --export-html=relatorio_auditoria.html</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>O arquivo HTML resultante possui aproximadamente 60KB, inclui todos os estilos, scripts e gráficos SVG embutidos, e pode ser aberto offline em qualquer navegador sem necessidade de conexão com a internet.</p>"
    },
    "cicd-sarif": {
      "id": "cicd-sarif",
      "title": "Integração com CI/CD, SARIF 2.1.0 & GitHub Actions",
      "category": "CI/CD & Segurança",
      "summary": "Automatize o fuzzer em pipelines de CI/CD: guia completo de uso do action.yml, exportação OASIS SARIF v2.1.0 para a aba Security/Code Scanning do GitHub, catálogo das 9 regras formais, relatórios JUnit XML e traces OpenTelemetry (OTLP JSON).",
      "content": "<h2>Segurança Contínua de Concorrência em CI/CD</h2>\n<p>Vulnerabilidades de concorrência em bancos de dados são categorizadas formalmente sob a classificação <strong>CWE-362 (Concurrent Execution using Shared Resource with Improper Synchronization)</strong>. No contexto corporativo, um bug de concorrência que permite saques duplicados ou reservas além do estoque não é apenas um defeito funcional — é uma falha de segurança financeira de alta severidade.</p>\n\n<p>O ChaosSQL permite transformar testes de isolamento de banco de dados em um portão de qualidade contínuo (<em>Quality Gate</em>), integrando-se nativamente com a aba <strong>Security / Code Scanning</strong> do GitHub via padrão internacional <strong>OASIS SARIF 2.1.0</strong>.</p>\n\n<h2>Uso da GitHub Action Oficial (<code>action.yml</code>)</h2>\n<p>Você pode adicionar o ChaosSQL aos seus workflows do GitHub Actions criando o arquivo <code>.github/workflows/concurrency_fuzzer.yml</code>:</p>\n\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <button class=\"copy-code-btn\" onclick=\"navigator.clipboard.writeText(document.getElementById('gh-workflow-code').innerText)\">Copiar</button>\n  <pre><code id=\"gh-workflow-code\" class=\"language-yaml\">name: Database Concurrency & Isolation Audit\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [ main ]\n  pull_request:\n    branches: [ main ]\n\njobs:\n  concurrency-audit:\n    name: ChaosSQL Isolation Gating\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    permissions:\n      security-events: write # Obrigatório para publicar alertas na aba Security\n      contents: read\n\n    steps:\n      - name: Checkout Repositório\n        uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Executar ChaosSQL Action\n        uses: bregaldahq/chaossql@v1\n        with:\n          spec-path: 'specs/banking_lost_update.yaml'\n          workers: 8\n          iterations: 50\n          seed: 42\n          export-junit: 'reports/chaossql-junit.xml'\n          export-summary: '$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY'\n          export-repro: 'true'\n\n      - name: Executar Auditoria com Exportação SARIF\n        if: always()\n        run: |\n          go run ./cmd/chaossql run specs/banking_lost_update.yaml \\\n            --export-sarif=reports/chaossql.sarif || true\n\n      - name: Publicar Alertas no GitHub Code Scanning\n        uses: github/codeql-action/upload-sarif@v3\n        if: always()\n        with:\n          sarif_file: reports/chaossql.sarif\n          category: chaossql-isolation-audit</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Mapeamento das 9 Regras do Catálogo OASIS SARIF 2.1.0</h2>\n<p>O módulo de geração SARIF do ChaosSQL (<code>internal/reporter/sarif.go</code>) emite relatórios no formato <code>Static Analysis Results Interchange Format (SARIF) v2.1.0</code>, associando cada violação a regras formais auditáveis:</p>\n\n<table class=\"matrix-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Rule ID</th>\n      <th>Nome Formal</th>\n      <th>Nível</th>\n      <th>Fenômeno e Ação de Remediação Recomendada</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/P4-lost-update</code></td>\n      <td>LostUpdateAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(239,68,68,0.2); color: #ef4444;\">error</span></td>\n      <td>Duas transações sobrescrevem o mesmo registro baseadas em leituras defasadas.<br><strong>Remediação:</strong> Elevar para <code>SERIALIZABLE</code>, usar <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> ou atualizar via incremento atômico no SQL.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/A5B-write-skew</code></td>\n      <td>WriteSkewAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(239,68,68,0.2); color: #ef4444;\">error</span></td>\n      <td>Transações com conjuntos de escrita disjuntos violam uma invariante global sobre conjuntos de leitura sobrepostos.<br><strong>Remediação:</strong> Habilitar Serializable Snapshot Isolation (SSI) ou lock de tabela/materializado.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/A5A-read-skew</code></td>\n      <td>ReadSkewAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(245,196,0,0.2); color: #f5c400;\">warning</span></td>\n      <td>Transação lê registro X, outra atualiza X e Y, e a primeira lê Y em estado inconsistente.<br><strong>Remediação:</strong> Elevar nível de isolamento para <code>REPEATABLE READ</code> ou <code>SNAPSHOT ISOLATION</code>.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/G0-dirty-write</code></td>\n      <td>DirtyWriteAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(239,68,68,0.2); color: #ef4444;\">error</span></td>\n      <td>Transação modifica dado já alterado por outra transação não confirmada.<br><strong>Remediação:</strong> Garantir bloqueios exclusivos de escrita durante toda a transação (Strict 2PL).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/G1a-dirty-read</code></td>\n      <td>DirtyReadAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(239,68,68,0.2); color: #ef4444;\">error</span></td>\n      <td>Transação lê dados de outra transação que sofreu rollback posterior.<br><strong>Remediação:</strong> Elevar nível de isolamento para <code>READ COMMITTED</code> ou superior.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/G1b-intermediate-read</code></td>\n      <td>IntermediateReadAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(239,68,68,0.2); color: #ef4444;\">error</span></td>\n      <td>Transação observa valor intermediário de outra transação ainda em andamento.<br><strong>Remediação:</strong> Reforçar barreiras de visibilidade atômica multiversão (MVCC).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/G1c-circular-info</code></td>\n      <td>CircularInfoAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(239,68,68,0.2); color: #ef4444;\">error</span></td>\n      <td>Ciclo de dependências mútuas de escrita e leitura entre transações.<br><strong>Remediação:</strong> Elevar para <code>SERIALIZABLE</code> para forçar ordenação acíclica no grafo de serialização.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/G2-anti-dependency</code></td>\n      <td>AntiDependencyCycle</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(239,68,68,0.2); color: #ef4444;\">error</span></td>\n      <td>Ciclo contendo arestas de anti-dependência ($rw$), violando serializabilidade estrita.<br><strong>Remediação:</strong> Ativar SSI (Serializable Snapshot Isolation) ou locks de predicado.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/G-DL-deadlock</code></td>\n      <td>DeadlockCycleAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(245,196,0,0.2); color: #f5c400;\">warning</span></td>\n      <td>Bloqueio mútuo circular entre transações concorrentes provocando aborts.<br><strong>Remediação:</strong> Adotar ordenação canônica estrita na aquisição de locks (ex: ordenar por ID crescente).</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Relatórios JUnit XML &amp; GitHub Step Summary</h2>\n<p>Além do SARIF, o ChaosSQL gera artefatos específicos para visualização imediata nos painéis de CI:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>JUnit XML (<code>--export-junit=junit.xml</code>):</strong> Gera relatórios consumidos pelo GitHub Actions Test Reporter, GitLab CI e Azure Pipelines. Falhas de concorrência são mapeadas para tags <code>&lt;failure&gt;</code> com a classificação da anomalia e diagrama Mermaid do ciclo.</li>\n  <li><strong>GitHub Step Summary (<code>--export-summary=$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY</code>):</strong> Renderiza tabelas formatadas em Markdown com as métricas da execução, anomalias encontradas e o código minimal reproduzível gerado pelo $ddmin$ diretamente na página do workflow do GitHub Actions.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Observabilidade com OpenTelemetry (OTLP JSON)</h2>\n<p>Com a flag <code>--export-otel=trace_otel.json</code>, o ChaosSQL converte toda a timeline de concorrência em um trace distribuído compatível com a especificação OpenTelemetry Tracing API. Cada worker goroutine é representado como um Span pai, e cada query SQL como um Span filho com atributos semânticos padronizados (<code>db.system</code>, <code>db.statement</code>, <code>db.operation</code>, <code>thread.id</code>). O arquivo pode ser ingerido diretamente em instâncias do Jaeger, Grafana Tempo ou Datadog para análise avançada de latência e concorrência.</p>"
    },
    "drivers": {
      "id": "drivers",
      "title": "Motores Suportados & Arquitetura de Drivers",
      "category": "Motores",
      "summary": "Guia de configuração e particularidades técnicas dos bancos de dados suportados: SQLite puro em Go (CGO=0 com WAL mode), PostgreSQL (pgx, READ COMMITTED vs SSI) e MySQL/MariaDB (InnoDB Repeatable Read e gap locks).",
      "content": "<h2>Arquitetura de Drivers e a Interface DatabaseDriver</h2>\n<p>O ChaosSQL interage com bancos de dados relacionais através de uma interface abstrata de alta performance definida em <code>internal/drivers/driver.go</code>:</p>\n\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-go\">type DatabaseDriver interface {\n    DriverName() string\n    Open(ctx context.Context) error\n    Close() error\n    Reset(ctx context.Context, schemaSQL, seedSQL string) error\n    BeginTx(ctx context.Context) (Tx, error)\n    QueryRow(ctx context.Context, query string, args ...any) *sql.Row\n    Query(ctx context.Context, query string, args ...any) (*sql.Rows, error)\n    Exec(ctx context.Context, query string, args ...any) (sql.Result, error)\n}</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>O método <code>Reset()</code> é fundamental para o fuzzer determinístico: antes de cada iteração de teste ou passo de teste do algoritmo de Delta-Debugging ($ddmin$), o ChaosSQL reinicializa completamente o banco de dados, garantindo isolamento total de estado entre execuções.</p>\n\n<h2>Driver SQLite Puro em Go (<code>modernc.org/sqlite</code>)</h2>\n\n<h3>Compilação Sem CGO (<code>CGO_ENABLED=0</code>)</h3>\n<p>Diferente de implementações baseadas em <code>mattn/go-sqlite3</code> que exigem compilador C e bibliotecas compartilhadas, o ChaosSQL utiliza o driver <code>modernc.org/sqlite</code>. Isso permite compilar binários estáticos universais com zero dependências externas de compilação.</p>\n\n<h3>Configuração WAL e Concorrência</h3>\n<p>Durante o método <code>Open()</code>, o driver aplica automaticamente as configurações de alta concorrência do SQLite:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- Habilita o modo Write-Ahead Logging para permitir leituras concorrentes com uma escrita\nPRAGMA journal_mode = WAL;\n\n-- Define o tempo de espera para aquisição de lock antes de falhar (5 segundos)\nPRAGMA busy_timeout = 5000;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Strings de Conexão Suportadas (DSN)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>:memory:</code> ou <code>file:chaos_mem?mode=memory&cache=shared</code>: Banco em memória volátil ultrarrápido (ideal para suítes de teste de alta velocidade, executando em &lt; 50ms).</li>\n  <li><code>file:database.db</code>: Banco persistido em arquivo local para testes de estresse em disco.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Driver PostgreSQL (<code>pgx/v5</code>)</h2>\n<p>O driver PostgreSQL do ChaosSQL utiliza o <code>github.com/jackc/pgx/v5/stdlib</code>, a biblioteca oficial de maior performance do ecossistema Go.</p>\n\n<h3>String de Conexão (DSN)</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>postgres://chaos_user:secret_pass@localhost:5432/chaostest?sslmode=disable</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Reset com Drop Cascade</h3>\n<p>Para garantir estado imaculado antes de cada teste, o driver PostgreSQL executa:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-sql\">DROP SCHEMA IF EXISTS public CASCADE;\nCREATE SCHEMA public;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Semântica de Isolamento: READ COMMITTED vs REPEATABLE READ vs SERIALIZABLE</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong><code>READ COMMITTED</code> (Padrão do PostgreSQL):</strong> Cada instrução SQL enxerga um snapshot gerado no início daquela instrução específica (<em>statement-level snapshot</em>). É altamente vulnerável a <strong>Lost Update ($P4$)</strong> e <strong>Read Skew ($A5A$)</strong> se a aplicação realizar lógica Read-Modify-Write.</li>\n  <li><strong><code>REPEATABLE READ</code>:</strong> Implementa Snapshot Isolation (SI). A transação inteira enxerga um snapshot consistente capturado no instante da primeira instrução. Previne $P4$ e $A5A$, mas permanece vulnerável a <strong>Write Skew ($A5B$)</strong>, pois transações com escritas disjuntas não colidem no motor de detecção de conflitos de escrita.</li>\n  <li><strong><code>SERIALIZABLE</code>:</strong> Ativa o algoritmo <strong>SSI (Serializable Snapshot Isolation)</strong> de Ports &amp; Grittner (VLDB 2012). O motor monitora locks virtuais de leitura (<code>SIREAD</code>) e aborta transações que participam de ciclos de anti-dependência com o erro SQLSTATE <code>40001 (serialization_failure)</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Tratamento de Deadlocks no PostgreSQL</h3>\n<p>O driver intercepta o erro SQLSTATE <code>40P01 (deadlock_detected)</code> e o remapeia automaticamente para o tipo de erro canônico <code>ErrDeadlockDetected</code>, categorizado pelo ChaosSQL sob a anomalia <code>G-DL</code>.</p>\n\n<h2>Driver MySQL &amp; MariaDB (<code>go-sql-driver/mysql</code>)</h2>\n<p>O driver MySQL utiliza <code>github.com/go-sql-driver/mysql</code> configurado com suporte a múltiplas instruções (<code>multiStatements=true</code>).</p>\n\n<h3>String de Conexão (DSN)</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>root:password@tcp(127.0.0.1:3306)/chaostest?parseTime=true&multiStatements=true</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Particularidades do Motor de Armazenamento InnoDB</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Nível Padrão <code>REPEATABLE READ</code>:</strong> Diferente do PostgreSQL, o InnoDB adota <code>REPEATABLE READ</code> como nível padrão.</li>\n  <li><strong>Next-Key Locks e Gap Locks:</strong> O InnoDB bloqueia não apenas os registros encontrados, mas também os intervalos vazios (<em>gaps</em>) entre eles em índices ordenados, impedindo a ocorrência de leituras fantasmas ($A3$).</li>\n  <li><strong>Deadlocks em Bloqueios Pessimistas:</strong> Transações concorrentes que executam <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> sobre linhas com chaves secundárias são propensas a deadlocks de intervalo (Gap Deadlocks). O ChaosSQL captura o erro MySQL <code>1213 (ER_LOCK_DEADLOCK)</code> e categoriza a anomalia.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Fuzzing Diferencial Cross-Engine com <code>chaossql diff</code></h2>\n<p>O comando <code>diff</code> executa o mesmo cenário com a mesma semente PRNG contra dois motores diferentes simultaneamente, emitindo um relatório comparativo de divergência semântica:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql diff specs/banking_lost_update.yaml \\\n  --driver-a=sqlite --dsn-a=\":memory:\" \\\n  --driver-b=postgres --dsn-b=\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/chaostest\" \\\n  --seed=42</code></pre>\n</div>"
    },
    "go-sdk": {
      "id": "go-sdk",
      "title": "Go Developer Testing SDK (pkg/chaostest)",
      "category": "SDK",
      "summary": "Como integrar o ChaosSQL diretamente em suítes de testes unitários do Go (*testing.T): referência completa da API fluente do builder chaostest.New(), asserções de invariantes e síntese automática de repro_test.go.",
      "content": "<h2>Introdução ao SDK (<code>pkg/chaostest</code>)</h2>\n<p>O <strong>ChaosSQL Go Developer Testing SDK</strong> (<code>pkg/chaostest</code>) foi desenvolvido para engenheiros de software que desejam incorporar testes de concorrência e invariantes diretamente nas suas suítes de testes unitários em Go (<code>*testing.T</code>). Ele permite testar camadas de persistência, repositórios e serviços de domínio diretamente via comando nativo <code>go test ./...</code>, com suporte completo a <code>go test -race</code> e sem a necessidade de instalar ou gerenciar binários externos.</p>\n\n<h2>API Fluente do Builder: <code>chaostest.New(t)</code></h2>\n<p>O pacote expõe um padrão builder fluente com encadeamento de métodos para configurar o cenário de concorrência de forma intuitiva:</p>\n\n<table class=\"matrix-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Método</th>\n      <th>Parâmetros</th>\n      <th>Descrição Técnica</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>New(t testing.TB)</code></td>\n      <td><code>testing.TB</code></td>\n      <td>Inicializa uma nova instância de teste atrelada ao ciclo de vida do teste atual.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>WithDriver(driver)</code></td>\n      <td><code>drivers.DatabaseDriver</code></td>\n      <td>Define a instância do driver. Se omitido, provisiona automaticamente um SQLite in-memory isolado.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>WithSchema(sql)</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Define os comandos SQL DDL para criação de tabelas e índices.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>WithSeed(sql)</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Define os comandos SQL DML para carga inicial de dados antes do teste.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>WithInvariant(name, query, expr)</code></td>\n      <td><code>string, string, string</code></td>\n      <td>Registra uma regra de negócio que deve ser preservada ao final do teste.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>WithJitter(minMs, maxMs)</code></td>\n      <td><code>int, int</code></td>\n      <td>Configura a faixa de jitter microtemporal injetada entre os passos de cada transação (padrão: 1ms a 5ms).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>AddOperation(name, steps...)</code></td>\n      <td><code>string, ...string</code></td>\n      <td>Registra uma transação concorrente. Suporta sintaxe de captura <code>-&gt; var</code> e expressões <code>{var - 100}</code>.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>AddOperationWithParams(name, params, steps...)</code></td>\n      <td><code>string, map[string]string, ...string</code></td>\n      <td>Registra uma operação com parâmetros gerados dinamicamente por transação.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Asserções de Teste: <code>AssertNoAnomalies</code> vs <code>AssertAnomalyDetected</code></h2>\n\n<h3>1. <code>AssertNoAnomalies(ctx, workers, iterations, seed)</code></h3>\n<p>Asserção positiva: valida que a implementação do serviço ou repositório é 100% segura contra corridas concorrentes sob as condições configuradas. Se uma anomalia for detectada ou uma invariante for violada, o método invoca <code>t.Fatalf</code> reportando:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Nome e valor da invariante que falhou.</li>\n  <li>Classificação formal da anomalia no catálogo Adya (ex: <code>P4_LOST_UPDATE</code>).</li>\n  <li>Métricas do Causal Delta-Debugging ($ddmin$): redução de $N$ operações originais para o caso minimal.</li>\n  <li>Diagrama de Sequência Mermaid formatado pronto para copiar.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. <code>AssertAnomalyDetected(ctx, workers, iterations, seed, expectedAnomaly...)</code></h3>\n<p>Asserção negativa: utilizada em testes de regressão para provar empiricamente que um código vulnerável ou um nível de isolamento insuficiente realmente dispara a anomalia prevista. Garante que o teste não apresente falsos negativos.</p>\n\n<h2>Exemplo Completo: Testando e Mitigando Lost Update</h2>\n<p>O exemplo abaixo demonstra como escrever um teste unitário em Go que comprova a eficácia de uma mitigação concorrente (migração de Read-Modify-Write vulnerável para um UPDATE atômico com guarda):</p>\n\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <button class=\"copy-code-btn\" onclick=\"navigator.clipboard.writeText(document.getElementById('go-sdk-code').innerText)\">Copiar</button>\n  <pre><code id=\"go-sdk-code\" class=\"language-go\">package bank_test\n\nimport (\n    \"context\"\n    \"testing\"\n\n    \"github.com/bregaldahq/chaossql/internal/drivers\"\n    \"github.com/bregaldahq/chaossql/pkg/chaostest\"\n)\n\n// TestAccountWithdrawal_AtomicMitigation valida que saques concorrentes\n// usando update atômico impedem a ocorrência de Lost Update (P4).\nfunc TestAccountWithdrawal_AtomicMitigation(t *testing.T) {\n    ctx := context.Background()\n\n    // 1. Instanciar driver de banco de dados (SQLite puro em memória)\n    driver := drivers.NewSQLiteDriver(\"file:bank_test?mode=memory&cache=shared\")\n    defer driver.Close()\n\n    // 2. Construir o fuzzer de concorrência com o SDK fluente\n    tester := chaostest.New(t).\n        WithDriver(driver).\n        WithSchema(`\n            CREATE TABLE accounts (\n                id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n                balance INT NOT NULL\n            );\n            CREATE TABLE ledger (\n                id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n                amount INT NOT NULL\n            );\n        `).\n        WithSeed(\"INSERT INTO accounts (id, balance) VALUES (1, 1000);\").\n        WithInvariant(\n            \"ledger_balance_consistency\",\n            \"SELECT (SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1) AS actual, \" +\n            \"(1000 - COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0)) AS expected FROM ledger;\",\n            \"actual == expected\",\n        ).\n        WithJitter(1, 4).\n        // Transação mitigada: UPDATE atômico com verificação de saldo suficiente\n        AddOperation(\"atomic_withdrawal\",\n            \"UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1 AND balance >= 100;\",\n            \"INSERT INTO ledger (amount) VALUES (100);\",\n        )\n\n    // 3. Executar o fuzzer com 8 workers simultâneos e 40 transações\n    // Deve passar com 100% de sucesso sem violação de invariantes\n    tester.AssertNoAnomalies(ctx, 8, 40, 42)\n}</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Síntese Automática de <code>repro_test.go</code> pelo $ddmin$</h2>\n<p>Quando um teste executado via SDK falha com <code>AssertNoAnomalies</code>, o algoritmo Causal Delta-Debugging ($ddmin$) não apenas isola o subconjunto de 2 operações mínimas, mas também sintetiza no log do teste o código fonte completo em Go de um arquivo <code>repro_test.go</code> independente.</p>\n<p>Esse código gerado pode ser colado em um arquivo local para depuração com breakpoints no Delve, permitindo que a equipe reproduza o defeito em 5 milissegundos sem qualquer dependência do fuzzer.</p>\n\n<div class=\"docs-callout tip\">\n  <strong>Determinismo Estrito em Testes de CI:</strong> Ao configurar testes de regressão no Go, utilize sempre uma semente estática constante (ex: <code>seed: 42</code>). Isso garante que o <code>go test</code> rode de forma ultrarrápida, previsível e sem oscilações (<em>flakiness</em>) no CI.\n</div>"
    },
    "academic-theory": {
      "id": "academic-theory",
      "title": "Teoria Formal & Fundamentação Matemática",
      "category": "Teoria",
      "summary": "O embasamento científico rigoroso do ChaosSQL: Condições de Conflito de Bernstein, Teorema da Serializabilidade por Conflito (CSR), agendamento probabilístico PCT com Teorema de Burckhardt, minimização causal com Zeller ddmin e verificação de linearizabilidade estilo Elle.",
      "content": "<h2>1. Modelo Formal de Transações e Histórias de Execução</h2>\n<p>O ChaosSQL fundamenta sua análise na teoria formal clássica de banco de dados estabelecida por Bernstein, Goodman, Papadimitriou e Adya.</p>\n\n<h3>Definição Formal de Transação</h3>\n<p>Uma transação $T_i$ é definida matematicamente como uma tupla ordenada:</p>\n<p>$$T_i = (O_i, <_i)$$</p>\n<p>Onde:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>$O_i$ é um conjunto finito de operações atômicas de leitura $r_i[x]$, escrita $w_i[x]$, commit $c_i$ ou abort $a_i$, acessando entidades de dados $x \\in \\mathcal{D}$.</li>\n  <li>$<_i$ é uma ordem estrita parcial sobre $O_i$ que reflete o fluxo determinístico do programa da transação.</li>\n  <li>Existe exatamente uma operação terminal $t_i \\in \\{a_i, c_i\\}$, que é o elemento maximal sob $<_i$.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Definição Formal de História (Schedule)</h3>\n<p>Uma história de execução concorrente $S$ sobre um conjunto finito de transações $\\mathcal{T} = \\{T_1, T_2, \\dots, T_n\\}$ é uma ordem parcial $S = (\\bigcup_{i=1}^n O_i, <_S)$ que satisfaz:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>$\\bigcup_{i=1}^n <_i \\;\\subseteq\\; <_S$ (preserva a ordem interna de instruções de cada transação).</li>\n  <li>Para quaisquer duas operações conflitantes $o_i, o_j \\in S$ (onde $i \\neq j$), ou $o_i <_S o_j$ ou $o_j <_S o_i$.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>2. Condições de Conflito de Bernstein &amp; Teorema CSR</h2>\n\n<h3>Condição de Conflito de Bernstein (1966)</h3>\n<p>Duas operações $o_i, o_j \\in S$ pertencentes a transações distintas ($i \\neq j$) estão em <strong>conflito direto</strong> se e somente se acessam a mesma entidade de dados $x \\in \\mathcal{D}$ e pelo menos uma delas é uma operação de mutação ($w$):</p>\n<p>$$\\\\text{Conflict}(o_i, o_j) \\\\iff (\\\\text{target}(o_i) = \\\\text{target}(o_j) = x) \\\\;\\\\land\\\\; (o_i \\\\text{ is } w \\\\;\\\\lor\\\\; o_j \\\\text{ is } w)$$</p>\n\n<h3>Grafo de Serialização Direta (Adya Dependency Graph)</h3>\n<p>Dado um schedule de execução $S$, o Grafo de Serialização $SG(S) = (V, E)$ é um dígrafo onde:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Os vértices $V = \\\\mathcal{T}$ correspondem ao conjunto de transações comitadas.</li>\n  <li>Existe uma aresta direcionada $(T_i \\\\to T_j) \\\\in E$ se e somente se existe $o_i \\\\in T_i$ e $o_j \\\\in T_j$ tais que $o_i <_S o_j$ e $\\\\text{Conflict}(o_i, o_j)$.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>As Três Relações Fundamentais de Dependência (Atul Adya, 1999)</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Dependência de Leitura ($wr$ - Read Dependency):</strong> $T_i \\\\xrightarrow{wr} T_j$ se $T_i$ grava uma versão de $x$ e $T_j$ subsequentemente lê essa mesma versão.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dependência de Sobrescrita ($ww$ - Overwrite Dependency):</strong> $T_i \\\\xrightarrow{ww} T_j$ se $T_i$ grava uma versão de $x$ e $T_j$ subsequentemente sobrescreve $x$.</li>\n  <li><strong>Anti-Dependência ($rw$ - Anti-Dependency):</strong> $T_i \\\\xrightarrow{rw} T_j$ se $T_i$ lê uma versão de $x$ e $T_j$ subsequentemente grava uma nova versão de $x$, tornando a leitura de $T_i$ desatualizada.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Teorema Fundamental da Serializabilidade por Conflito (CSR)</h3>\n<div class=\"docs-callout info\">\n  <strong>Teorema Fundamental:</strong> Uma história concorrente $S$ é Serializavel por Conflito ($S \\\\in \\\\text{CSR}$) se e somente se seu Grafo de Serialização $SG(S)$ é um <strong>Grafo Direcionado Acíclico (DAG)</strong>.\n</div>\n<p>A presença de qualquer ciclo direcionado ($T_1 \\\\to T_2 \\\\to \\\\dots \\\\to T_1$) prova que a execução é semanticamente inconsistente e não possui equivalente serial.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Agendamento Probabilístico PCT-SQL (Burckhardt &amp; Musuvathi)</h2>\n<p>Testes de concorrência ingênuos falham devido à explosão combinatória. Para $n$ transações executando $m$ passos cada, o número de intercalações possíveis $\\\\Omega$ cresce fatorialmente:</p>\n<p>$$|\\\\Omega| = \\\\frac{(n \\\\cdot m)!}{(m!)^n}$$</p>\n<p>Para apenas 5 transações de 3 passos, existem <strong>168.168.000 intercalações possíveis</strong>. A chance de acertar a troca de contexto exata por pura aleatoriedade é inferior a $10^{-6}$.</p>\n\n<h3>Teorema do Limite Inferior de Burckhardt (ASPLOS 2010)</h3>\n<p>O ChaosSQL adota o algoritmo <em>Probabilistic Concurrency Testing</em> (PCT). Burckhardt e Musuvathi provaram que, se um bug de concorrência requer uma profundidade de agendamento $d$ (número de restrições de prioridade ou trocas de contexto críticas para disparar), o algoritmo garante probabilidade mínima de detecção em uma única rodada:</p>\n<p>$$\\\\mathbb{P}(\\\\text{Detecção}) \\\\ge \\\\frac{1}{n \\\\cdot k^{d-1}}$$</p>\n<p>Onde $n$ é o número de threads concorrentes e $k$ é o total de passos executados.</p>\n\n<h3>Aplicação Prática em Bancos de Dados Relacionais</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Estudos empíricos seminais (Lu et al., ASPLOS) demonstram que <strong>~96% dos bugs de concorrência reais possuem profundidade $d = 1$ ou $d = 2$</strong> (Lost Update requer $d=1$; Write Skew requer $d=2$).</li>\n  <li>Para $d = 2$, a probabilidade de detecção por execução é $\\\\mathbb{P} \\\\ge \\\\frac{1}{n \\\\cdot k}$. Em um teste com 4 workers e 25 passos ($n=4, k=25$), $\\\\mathbb{P} \\\\ge \\\\frac{1}{100} = 1\\\\%$.</li>\n  <li>Em uma bateria curta de apenas $R = 300$ iterações (que executa em &lt; 1 segundo em memória):</li>\n</ul>\n<p>$$\\\\mathbb{P}(\\\\text{Encontrar o Bug em } R \\\\text{ Rodadas}) = 1 - \\\\left(1 - \\\\frac{1}{100}\\\\right)^{300} \\\\approx 1 - e^{-3} \\\\approx \\\\mathbf{95.02\\\\%}$$</p>\n\n<h2>4. Minimização Causal com Andreas Zeller Delta-Debugging ($ddmin$)</h2>\n\n<h3>Definição Formal de $1$-Minimalidade</h3>\n<p>Seja $C = \\\\langle op_1, op_2, \\\\dots, op_N \\\\rangle$ a sequência ordenada de operações agendadas que dispara uma falha de invariante $\\\\text{test}(C) = \\\\text{FAIL}$.</p>\n<p>Uma subsequência $C^* \\\\subseteq C$ é dita <strong>$1$-Minimal</strong> se e somente se:</p>\n<p>$$\\\\text{test}(C^*) = \\\\text{FAIL} \\\\quad \\\\land \\\\quad \\\\forall op \\\\in C^*: \\\\text{test}(C^* \\\\setminus \\\\{op\\\\}) = \\\\text{PASS}$$</p>\n\n<h3>O Algoritmo Causal-$ddmin$ do ChaosSQL</h3>\n<p>Em bancos de dados relacionais, o particionamento binário ingênuo do $ddmin$ clássico falha porque remover uma operação que gerou um registro pai provoca erros artificiais de chave estrangeira (<code>FOREIGN KEY constraint failed</code>).</p>\n<p>O ChaosSQL resolve isso através do <strong>Causal Delta-Debugging</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Antes de testar um subconjunto particionado $C' \\\\subset C$, o motor computa o Grafo de Causalidade de Dados.</li>\n  <li>Calcula o fecho causal $\\\\text{Closure}(C')$: se $T_j \\\\in C'$ consome uma chave criada por $T_i$, $T_i$ é compulsoriamente incluído no teste.</li>\n  <li>Isso elimina 100% dos falsos positivos por integridade referencial e acelera a convergência em até <strong>$4\\\\times$</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>5. Linearizabilidade e Observações de Caixa-Preta (Elle)</h2>\n<p>Inspirado na metodologia de Kyle Kingsbury (Jepsen) e Peter Alvaro (VLDB 2020), o ChaosSQL infere anomalias estritamente a partir do histórico observável de operações do cliente (leituras, escritas e retornos). Não há necessidade de instrumentação interna no código fonte do banco de dados, tornando o ChaosSQL universalmente compatível com qualquer motor relacional compatível com SQL.</p>"
    }
  },
  "en": {
    "getting-started": {
      "id": "getting-started",
      "title": "Quickstart Guide & Core Architecture",
      "category": "Getting Started",
      "summary": "Complete guide to installing, configuring, and executing your first deterministic database race condition stress test with zero CGO dependencies.",
      "content": "<h2>What is ChaosSQL?</h2>\n<p><strong>ChaosSQL</strong> is a Deterministic Concurrency and Invariant Fuzzer for SQL Databases, designed by Ricardo Bregalda (Studio Bregalda). In modern concurrent systems and distributed microservices architectures, concurrency anomalies—such as <em>Lost Updates</em>, <em>Write Skew</em>, <em>Phantom Reads</em>, and transient <em>Deadlocks</em>—are notoriously elusive to reproduce, isolate, and debug. Traditional unit tests execute sequentially in isolation, whereas stochastic load-testing tools (such as JMeter or k6) inject uncontrollable scheduling noise that rarely hits the precise microsecond context switches required to trigger race conditions.</p>\n\n<p>ChaosSQL bridges this fundamental gap by combining chaos engineering with three core pillars of theoretical computer science:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Stochastic Probabilistic Scheduling (PCT-SQL):</strong> Based on the seminal <em>Probabilistic Concurrency Testing</em> algorithm by Burckhardt &amp; Musuvathi (ASPLOS 2010), providing formal mathematical guarantees on the lower bound of detection probability across exponential schedule spaces.</li>\n  <li><strong>Automated Anomaly Classification via Adya Graphs:</strong> Reconstructs the Direct Serialization Graph (DSG) based on Atul Adya's MIT 1999 doctoral thesis and black-box verification techniques inspired by Elle (VLDB 2020), identifying $rw$ (anti-dependency), $ww$ (overwrite), and $wr$ (read dependency) conflict edges.</li>\n  <li><strong>Causal Delta-Debugging ($ddmin$):</strong> Implements Andreas Zeller's delta-debugging algorithm enhanced with causal closure and referential integrity preservation, shrinking hundreds of chaotic operations down to a <em>1-minimal</em> counterexample of just 2 operations in milliseconds.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"docs-callout info\">\n  <strong>Zero CGO &amp; Pure Go:</strong> ChaosSQL compiles into a single static executable binary with zero external C dependencies (<code>CGO_ENABLED=0</code>). The embedded SQLite engine runs via a native pure-Go transpiler (<code>modernc.org/sqlite</code>), enabling instant execution inside any Alpine container, WSL2, Linux, macOS, or Windows environment.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Core 4-Stage Architecture Pipeline</h2>\n<p>Every execution of ChaosSQL flows through a modular, deterministic four-stage processing pipeline:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Fuzzer (PCT-SQL Scheduler):</strong> Orchestrates concurrent worker goroutines with calibrated microsecond jitter and dynamic priority assignment, exploring non-linear execution schedules.</li>\n  <li><strong>Detector (Adya Cycle &amp; Invariant Engine):</strong> Evaluates data consistency assertions, inspects transactional histories, and constructs the Direct Serialization Graph ($SG(S)$) to identify anomalous cycles ($rw, ww, wr$).</li>\n  <li><strong>Shrinker (Causal Delta-Debugging $ddmin$):</strong> Applies causal graph reduction to prune non-essential operations while preserving referential integrity, isolating a minimal 2-operation reproduction.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reporter (Multi-Format Audit Dispatcher):</strong> Formats findings into OASIS SARIF 2.1.0, JUnit XML, Markdown Step Summaries, OpenTelemetry OTLP JSON, interactive HTML reports, and Mermaid sequence diagrams.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>System Requirements &amp; Installation</h2>\n<p>ChaosSQL requires only Go 1.22 or higher (if building from source) or can be downloaded directly as a standalone precompiled binary.</p>\n\n<h3>Method 1: Quick Install via Go Toolchain</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <button class=\"copy-code-btn\" onclick=\"navigator.clipboard.writeText('go install github.com/bregaldahq/chaossql/cmd/chaossql@latest')\">Copy</button>\n  <pre><code>go install github.com/bregaldahq/chaossql/cmd/chaossql@latest</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Method 2: Static Compilation from Git Repository</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <button class=\"copy-code-btn\" onclick=\"navigator.clipboard.writeText('git clone https://github.com/bregaldahq/chaossql.git\\ncd chaossql\\nCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags=\\\"-s -w\\\" -o bin/chaossql ./cmd/chaossql')\">Copy</button>\n  <pre><code># 1. Clone the official repository\ngit clone https://github.com/bregaldahq/chaossql.git\ncd chaossql\n\n# 2. Compile high-performance static binary\nCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o bin/chaossql ./cmd/chaossql\n\n# 3. Verify installation\n./bin/chaossql --help</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Method 3: Execution via Docker Container</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>docker run --rm -v $(pwd):/workspace -w /workspace ghcr.io/bregaldahq/chaossql:latest run specs/banking.yaml</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Your First Test in 5 Minutes (Step-by-Step)</h2>\n<p>Let us create and test a classic banking lost update scenario (anomaly $P4$), where two concurrent clients withdraw funds simultaneously from the same account under permissive transaction isolation.</p>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Create Scaffolding with <code>chaossql init</code></h3>\n<p>The <code>init</code> subcommand scaffolds a complete scenario directory with ready-to-run template files:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql init quickstart_banking --driver sqlite</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>The generated directory structure contains:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>schema.sql</code>: Table schemas and DDL index definitions.</li>\n  <li><code>seed.sql</code>: Initial DML dataset seeding.</li>\n  <li><code>chaos.yaml</code>: Declarative specification for database engine, workers, invariants, and operations.</li>\n  <li><code>README.md</code>: Scenario contextual documentation.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Define the DDL Schema (<code>schema.sql</code>)</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- Bank accounts table\nCREATE TABLE accounts (\n    id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    owner TEXT NOT NULL,\n    balance INT NOT NULL\n);</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Seed Initial Data (<code>seed.sql</code>)</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- Initial balance of $1,000 for Alice\nINSERT INTO accounts (id, owner, balance) VALUES (1, 'Alice', 1000);</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 4: Configure the Specification (<code>chaos.yaml</code>)</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">version: \"1.0\"\nname: \"quickstart_banking\"\ndescription: \"Verify balance integrity under concurrent withdrawals\"\n\ndatabase:\n  driver: \"sqlite\"\n  schema: \"schema.sql\"\n  seed: \"seed.sql\"\n\nengine:\n  workers: 4          # 4 concurrent goroutines\n  iterations: 20      # 20 withdrawal operations in total\n  seed: 42            # Deterministic reproducible PRNG seed\n  jitter_ms: [1, 5]   # Stochastic micro-delays between 1ms and 5ms\n\ninvariants:\n  - name: \"total_balance_consistency\"\n    query: \"SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1;\"\n    assert: \"balance == 1000 - (total_completed * 100)\"\n\noperations:\n  - name: \"withdraw_100\"\n    steps:\n      - \"SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1 -> cur\"\n      - \"UPDATE accounts SET balance = {cur - 100} WHERE id = 1\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 5: Run the Fuzzer with <code>chaossql run</code></h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code># Run by iteration count\nchaossql run quickstart_banking/chaos.yaml --seed=42 --workers=4\n\n# Or execute with time-bounded duration\nchaossql run quickstart_banking/chaos.yaml --workers=4 --duration=10s</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 6: Analyzing Terminal Diagnostics</h3>\n<p>ChaosSQL executes all 4 workers concurrently, interleaving read (<code>SELECT</code>) and write (<code>UPDATE</code>) operations with calibrated stochastic jitter. Within milliseconds, the fuzzer catches the invariant breakdown:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Detected Violation:</strong> <code>balance == 1000 - (total_completed * 100)</code> failed (expected: 0, actual: 500).</li>\n  <li><strong>Adya Classification:</strong> Anomaly <code>P4_LOST_UPDATE</code> ($G\\text{-single}$ cycle).</li>\n  <li><strong>Conflict Graph:</strong> <code>T1 ──(rw)──► T2 ──(ww)──► T1</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Causal Delta-Debugging ($ddmin$):</strong> Shrunk original 20-operation execution trace to just 2 critical operations in 68ms (90.0% reduction).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"docs-callout tip\">\n  <strong>Interactive Visual Interface:</strong> Pass the <code>--ui</code> flag during execution (e.g., <code>chaossql run chaos.yaml --ui</code>) to immediately spin up the local visualizer at <code>http://127.0.0.1:8090</code>, featuring interactive microsecond Gantt swimlanes and SVG dependency graphs.\n</div>\n\n<h2>Flagship Built-in Demonstrations</h2>\n<p>ChaosSQL bundles 10 production-grade reference scenarios ready for instant execution via the <code>chaossql demo</code> subcommand:</p>\n\n<table class=\"matrix-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Command</th>\n      <th>Phenomenon</th>\n      <th>Adya Code</th>\n      <th>Severity</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo banking</code></td>\n      <td>Banking Lost Update</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">P4</span></td>\n      <td>High (Mutation Loss)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo inventory</code></td>\n      <td>Inventory Oversell</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">A3</span></td>\n      <td>Critical (Negative Stock)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo hospital</code></td>\n      <td>Hospital Write Skew</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">A5B</span></td>\n      <td>Critical (Global Invariant Violation)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo financial</code></td>\n      <td>Financial Read Skew</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">A5A</span></td>\n      <td>Medium (Inconsistent Audit View)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo auction</code></td>\n      <td>Auction Dirty Write</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">G0</span></td>\n      <td>Critical (2PL Breakdown)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo crypto</code></td>\n      <td>Crypto Arbitrage Info Cycle</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">G1c</span></td>\n      <td>High (Circular Information Flow)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo flash_crash</code></td>\n      <td>Flash Crash Dirty Read</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">G1a</span></td>\n      <td>Critical (Aborted Read Exposure)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo ticket</code></td>\n      <td>Ticket Anti-Dependency</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">G2</span></td>\n      <td>High (Overlapping Overbooking)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo deadlock</code></td>\n      <td>Deadlock Cycle &amp; Recovery</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">G-DL</span></td>\n      <td>Medium (Mutual Lock Contention)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql demo fk</code></td>\n      <td>Foreign Key Cascade Deadlock</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\">G-DL</span></td>\n      <td>High (Cascading Contention)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>"
    },
    "dsl-spec": {
      "id": "dsl-spec",
      "title": "Declarative Language Specification",
      "category": "Specification",
      "summary": "Exhaustive syntax reference for chaos.yaml: transaction definitions, dynamic expressions, variable bindings, and invariant assertions.",
      "content": "<h2>Overview of the Declarative Language (DSL)</h2>\n<p>ChaosSQL employs a declarative YAML-based Domain-Specific Language (<code>chaos.yaml</code>) to specify concurrency fuzzing scenarios entirely decoupled from application source code. The specification formally models target database configurations, worker concurrency scheduling, transactional sequences, inductive invariants, and stochastic fault injection rules.</p>\n\n<h2>Complete Root Properties Taxonomy</h2>\n<table class=\"matrix-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Field</th>\n      <th>Type</th>\n      <th>Required</th>\n      <th>Technical Description</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>version</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Yes</td>\n      <td>DSL specification schema version (supports <code>\"1.0\"</code> and <code>\"1.1\"</code>).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>name</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Yes</td>\n      <td>Unique scenario identifier (e.g., <code>banking_lost_update</code>).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>description</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>No</td>\n      <td>Textual documentation detailing concurrency hypothesis and business rules.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>database</code> (or <code>setup</code>)</td>\n      <td><code>object</code></td>\n      <td>Yes</td>\n      <td>Target engine driver configuration, DDL schema, and DML seed scripts.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>engine</code> (or <code>workload</code>)</td>\n      <td><code>object</code></td>\n      <td>Yes</td>\n      <td>Execution parameters: worker count, PRNG seed, and stochastic jitter ranges.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>operations</code> (or <code>transactions</code>)</td>\n      <td><code>array</code></td>\n      <td>Yes</td>\n      <td>Atomic transactional workloads scheduled across concurrent workers.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>invariants</code></td>\n      <td><code>array</code></td>\n      <td>Yes</td>\n      <td>Post-execution and continuous global data consistency assertions.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>faults</code> (or <code>fault_injection</code>)</td>\n      <td><code>object</code></td>\n      <td>No</td>\n      <td>Controlled stochastic fault injection (transient aborts, connection drops).</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>The <code>database</code> (or <code>setup</code>) Block</h2>\n<p>Specifies the target database driver, connection details, and initialization lifecycle scripts:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">database:\n  driver: \"sqlite\"             # Supported drivers: \"sqlite\", \"postgres\", \"mysql\"\n  schema: \"schema.sql\"         # Relative path to DDL schema script or inline SQL string\n  seed: \"seed.sql\"             # Relative path to DML initial seed data script\n  dsn: \"file:memdb?mode=memory&cache=shared\" # Optional DSN (required for Postgres/MySQL)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>The <code>engine</code> (or <code>workload</code>) Block</h2>\n<p>Configures concurrent thread scheduling and deterministic randomization:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">engine:\n  workers: 8                   # Number of concurrent worker goroutines\n  iterations: 50               # Total number of transactions to schedule (mutually exclusive with duration)\n  duration: 10s                # Optional: time-bounded execution duration\n  seed: 1337                   # Deterministic 64-bit PCG64 PRNG seed\n  jitter_ms: [1, 10]           # Stochastic delay interval [min, max] between SQL steps\n  isolation: \"READ COMMITTED\"  # Optional: transaction isolation level override</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Operations, Variable Capture, and Dynamic Expressions</h2>\n<p>Each entry in the <code>operations</code> (or <code>transactions</code>) array models an atomic transactional workload. Steps within an operation execute sequentially inside the transaction assigned to an individual worker.</p>\n\n<h3>Lexical Capture Syntax (<code>-&gt; var_name</code> or <code>=&gt; var_name</code>)</h3>\n<p>Extracts column values from a <code>SELECT</code> query result and binds them to lexical variables in the transaction context for subsequent statement interpolation:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">operations:\n  - name: \"transfer_funds\"\n    steps:\n      # Capture column 'balance' into variable 'current_bal'\n      - \"SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1 -> current_bal\"\n      - \"UPDATE accounts SET balance = {current_bal - 100} WHERE id = 1\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Dynamic Expression Interpolation (<code>{expr}</code>)</h3>\n<p>ChaosSQL embeds the high-performance <code>expr-lang</code> compilation engine, supporting arithmetic, logical conditions, and inline expressions evaluated at runtime:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Arithmetic Operations:</strong> <code>{current_bal - 100}</code>, <code>{price * quantity * 0.9}</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Ternary Conditionals:</strong> <code>{avail &gt; 0 ? avail - 1 : 0}</code></li>\n  <li><strong>Dynamic Variable Substitution:</strong> <code>UPDATE products SET stock = {avail - 1} WHERE id = {target_product}</code></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Conditional Guard Predicates in SQL</h3>\n<p>Dynamic expressions can be embedded inside <code>WHERE</code> clauses to model atomic compare-and-swap or guarded update logic:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">operations:\n  - name: \"conditional_checkout\"\n    steps:\n      - \"SELECT stock FROM products WHERE id = 1 -> avail\"\n      - \"UPDATE products SET stock = {avail - 1} WHERE id = 1 AND {avail > 0}\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Dynamic Parameters (<code>params</code>)</h3>\n<p>Version 1.1 of the DSL introduces randomized per-transaction parameter generators:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">operations:\n  - name: \"dynamic_transfer\"\n    params:\n      sender_id: \"rand_int(1, 10)\"\n      receiver_id: \"rand_int(11, 20)\"\n      amount: \"rand_int(50, 200)\"\n    steps:\n      - \"UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - {amount} WHERE id = {sender_id}\"\n      - \"UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + {amount} WHERE id = {receiver_id}\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Global Invariant Assertions (<code>invariants</code>)</h2>\n<p>Invariants represent system conservation laws and inductive safety properties that must hold true regardless of thread interleavings.</p>\n<table class=\"matrix-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Field</th>\n      <th>Type</th>\n      <th>Description</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>name</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Descriptive identifier for the invariant rule (e.g., <code>conservation_of_money</code>).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>query</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Aggregated verification SQL query executed post-fuzzing to retrieve final state.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>assert</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Boolean expression evaluated over the query result columns and engine metrics.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Supported Assertion Comparison Operators</h3>\n<p>Invariant assertions support all standard relational comparison operators evaluated over query columns:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>==</code> : Strict equality (e.g., <code>total == 2000</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>!=</code> : Non-equality (e.g., <code>drift != 0</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;</code> : Less than (e.g., <code>overdraft_count &lt; 1</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;=</code> : Less than or equal to (e.g., <code>active_claims &lt;= max_capacity</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>&gt;</code> : Greater than (e.g., <code>on_duty &gt; 0</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>&gt;=</code> : Greater than or equal to (e.g., <code>balance &gt;= 0</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Special Variables Injected into Assertion Context</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>col_name</code>: Values returned by any column in the invariant verification query (e.g., <code>total</code>, <code>balance</code>, <code>on_duty</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>total_completed</code>: Total number of transactions successfully committed (<code>EventCommit</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>total_aborted</code>: Total number of transactions rolled back due to serialization conflicts or deadlocks.</li>\n  <li><code>total_operations</code>: Absolute count of scheduled transactions.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">invariants:\n  # 1. Fundamental conservation of capital (strict equality: ==)\n  - name: \"conservation_of_money\"\n    query: \"SELECT sum(balance) AS total FROM accounts;\"\n    assert: \"total == 2000\"\n\n  # 2. Strict linear relationship between successful commits and balance\n  - name: \"balance_audit\"\n    query: \"SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1;\"\n    assert: \"balance == 1000 - (total_completed * 100)\"\n\n  # 3. Non-negativity constraint (greater than or equal to: >=)\n  - name: \"stock_non_negative\"\n    query: \"SELECT stock FROM products WHERE id = 1;\"\n    assert: \"stock >= 0\"\n\n  # 4. Hospital on-call safety quorum\n  - name: \"at_least_one_doctor_on_call\"\n    query: \"SELECT count(*) AS on_duty FROM doctors WHERE on_call = true;\"\n    assert: \"on_duty >= 1\"\n\n  # 5. Non-zero drift guard (inequality: !=)\n  - name: \"no_audit_drift\"\n    query: \"SELECT abs(sum(debit) - sum(credit)) AS drift FROM general_ledger;\"\n    assert: \"drift == 0\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Controlled Fault Injection (<code>faults</code> / <code>fault_injection</code>)</h2>\n<p>Allows engineers to stress isolation mechanisms under simulated network partitions, unhandled client disconnects, and ungraceful aborts:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">faults:\n  transient_abort_rate: 0.15  # 15% probability of abrupt mid-transaction ROLLBACK (tests G1a dirty read)\n  connection_drop_rate: 0.05  # 5% probability of sudden connection termination\n  latency_spikes:\n    probability: 0.10\n    delay_ms: 100             # Injects 100ms asynchronous pause to widen race vulnerability window</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"docs-callout warning\">\n  <strong>Mandatory Static Linter:</strong> Always validate complex specifications prior to execution using <code>chaossql validate &lt;spec.yaml&gt;</code>. The linter precompiles dynamic expressions and checks referential schema integrity to prevent runtime type errors.\n</div>"
    },
    "cli-reference": {
      "id": "cli-reference",
      "title": "CLI Manual, Subcommands & All 12 Flags",
      "category": "Interface",
      "summary": "Comprehensive manual for the chaossql command-line interface, detailed options, exit codes, and operational flags.",
      "content": "<h2>CLI Architecture &amp; Design Philosophy</h2>\n<p>The ChaosSQL Command-Line Interface (CLI) is engineered for systems developers who demand rapid feedback loops, granular scheduling control, and frictionless integration with automated CI/CD pipelines. Built upon Cobra and styled with Charm Lipgloss, the CLI outputs expressive ANSI terminal diagnostics, soft tonal colors from the Studio Bregalda Design System, and exportable industry-standard artifacts (SARIF, JUnit XML, standalone HTML, OpenTelemetry OTLP JSON, and Mermaid sequence diagrams).</p>\n\n<h2>Complete Table of Execution, Scheduling &amp; Export Flags</h2>\n<p>All flags can be passed to execution subcommands (<code>run</code> and <code>demo</code>), overriding corresponding parameters defined in the scenario specification file:</p>\n\n<table class=\"matrix-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Flag</th>\n      <th>Type</th>\n      <th>Default</th>\n      <th>Subcommands</th>\n      <th>Technical Description</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--driver</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"\"</code> (reads spec)</td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>diff</code>, <code>matrix</code>, <code>init</code></td>\n      <td>Target database engine driver (<code>sqlite</code>, <code>postgres</code>, <code>mysql</code>).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--dsn</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"\"</code> (reads spec)</td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>diff</code>, <code>matrix</code></td>\n      <td>Database connection string / Data Source Name for external database engines.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--config</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"\"</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>validate</code></td>\n      <td>Alternative file path to the YAML scenario specification.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--workers</code></td>\n      <td><code>int</code></td>\n      <td><code>0</code> (reads spec)</td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code>, <code>bench</code></td>\n      <td>Number of concurrent goroutines executing simultaneous transactions.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--duration</code></td>\n      <td><code>duration</code></td>\n      <td><code>0s</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code>, <code>bench</code></td>\n      <td>Time-bounded fuzzing duration (e.g., <code>10s</code>, <code>1m</code>). Mutually exclusive with iterations.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--iterations</code></td>\n      <td><code>int</code></td>\n      <td><code>0</code> (reads spec)</td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Total transaction iterations scheduled across all workers during the session.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--jitter-min</code></td>\n      <td><code>int</code></td>\n      <td><code>1</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Minimum stochastic jitter micro-delay in milliseconds injected between SQL steps.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--jitter-max</code></td>\n      <td><code>int</code></td>\n      <td><code>10</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Maximum stochastic jitter micro-delay in milliseconds injected between SQL steps.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--seed</code></td>\n      <td><code>uint64</code></td>\n      <td><code>0</code> (reads spec)</td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code>, <code>diff</code></td>\n      <td>Deterministic PCG64 PRNG seed for bit-for-bit identical anomaly reproduction.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--pct-depth</code></td>\n      <td><code>int</code></td>\n      <td><code>2</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Maximum schedule priority change depth $d$ for the PCT-SQL algorithm.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--ddmin</code></td>\n      <td><code>bool</code></td>\n      <td><code>true</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Enables or disables Causal Delta-Debugging counterexample minimization.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--output</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"\"</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code>, <code>matrix</code></td>\n      <td>Destination directory or base path for generated execution reports.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--port</code></td>\n      <td><code>int</code></td>\n      <td><code>8090</code></td>\n      <td><code>ui</code>, <code>run</code></td>\n      <td>Port for the local embedded HTTP trace visualizer web server.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--export-sarif</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"\"</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Exports findings in OASIS SARIF v2.1.0 format for GitHub Code Scanning.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--export-html</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"\"</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Generates a standalone, dependency-free interactive HTML audit report (~60KB).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--export-otel</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"\"</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Exports execution trace as OpenTelemetry OTLP JSON for distributed observability.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--export-junit</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"\"</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Exports execution results as standard JUnit XML for CI/CD test dashboards.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--export-summary</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"\"</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Exports a GitHub-flavored Markdown report directly to <code>$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY</code>.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--export-mermaid</code></td>\n      <td><code>bool</code></td>\n      <td><code>false</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Exports chronological sequence diagram of conflicting transactions (<code>trace.mermaid</code>).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--export-repro</code></td>\n      <td><code>bool</code></td>\n      <td><code>false</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Synthesizes an isolated Go regression test (<code>repro_test.go</code>) with $ddmin$ reduction.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--ui</code></td>\n      <td><code>bool</code></td>\n      <td><code>false</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code></td>\n      <td>Automatically launches the embedded trace visualizer on <code>127.0.0.1:8090</code>.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>--json</code></td>\n      <td><code>bool</code></td>\n      <td><code>false</code></td>\n      <td><code>run</code>, <code>demo</code>, <code>bench</code>, <code>diff</code>, <code>matrix</code></td>\n      <td>Outputs structured machine-readable JSON to stdout for external automation scripts.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Exhaustive Reference of the 9 Subcommands</h2>\n\n<h3>1. <code>chaossql run &lt;chaos.yaml&gt;</code></h3>\n<p>Executes concurrency and invariant fuzzing against the provided specification file:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code># Standard execution\nchaossql run specs/banking_lost_update.yaml\n\n# Execution with overrides and comprehensive multi-format report exports\nchaossql run specs/banking_lost_update.yaml \\\n  --seed=42 \\\n  --workers=8 \\\n  --iterations=50 \\\n  --export-sarif=reports/audit.sarif \\\n  --export-html=reports/report.html \\\n  --export-junit=reports/junit.xml \\\n  --export-repro \\\n  --ui</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>2. <code>chaossql demo [scenario]</code></h3>\n<p>Launches one of the 10 built-in reference demonstrations featuring terminal visualization and delta-debugging:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql demo banking       # P4 Lost Update\nchaossql demo inventory     # A3 Inventory Oversell\nchaossql demo hospital      # A5B Hospital Write Skew\nchaossql demo financial     # A5A Financial Read Skew\nchaossql demo auction       # G0 Auction Dirty Write\nchaossql demo crypto        # G1c Circular Information Flow\nchaossql demo flash_crash   # G1a Flash Crash Dirty Read\nchaossql demo ticket        # G2 Anti-Dependency Cycle\nchaossql demo deadlock      # G-DL Mutual Lock Deadlock\nchaossql demo fk            # Foreign Key Cascade Deadlock</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>3. <code>chaossql ui &lt;trace.json&gt;</code></h3>\n<p>Starts the embedded HTTP web visualizer to inspect a previously recorded execution trace:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql ui trace_dump.json --port=8090 --no-open</code></pre>\n</div>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>--port int</code>: Local HTTP listener port (default <code>8090</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>--no-open bool</code>: Disables automatic launch of the system default web browser.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. <code>chaossql diff &lt;spec.yaml&gt;</code></h3>\n<p>Executes cross-engine differential fuzzing, comparing the runtime isolation semantics of two different database engines under identical workloads and seeds:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql diff specs/lost_update.yaml \\\n  --driver-a=sqlite --dsn-a=\":memory:\" \\\n  --driver-b=postgres --dsn-b=\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/testdb\" \\\n  --seed=42</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>5. <code>chaossql replay &lt;result.json&gt;</code></h3>\n<p>Renders a microsecond-accurate terminal Gantt swimlane from a recorded execution trace:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql replay result.json --max-events=100</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>6. <code>chaossql bench</code></h3>\n<p>Executes throughput microbenchmarks measuring deterministic PRNG generation and Adya cycle detection speeds:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql bench --duration=3s --workers=8 --json</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>Benchmarks typically demonstrate throughput exceeding <strong>13.9 million operations/second</strong> on modern multi-core processors.</p>\n\n<h3>7. <code>chaossql validate &lt;chaos.yaml&gt;</code></h3>\n<p>Static linter that verifies YAML schema adherence, compiles invariant dynamic expressions, and validates relational referential integrity before runtime execution:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql validate specs/hospital_write_skew.yaml</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>8. <code>chaossql init &lt;path&gt;</code></h3>\n<p>Generates production-ready scaffolding for a new test scenario with pre-configured templates:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql init new_scenario --driver=postgres --name=balance_audit --force</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>9. <code>chaossql matrix</code></h3>\n<p>Executes an empirical matrix validation based on Martin Kleppmann's Hermitage test taxonomy, testing the target engine against all 9 formal isolation anomalies:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql matrix --driver=postgres --dsn=\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/testdb\" --markdown</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Process Exit Codes</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>0</code>: <strong>Clean / Safe</strong>. All transactions completed successfully and all invariant assertions passed with zero anomalies.</li>\n  <li><code>1</code>: <strong>Anomaly Detected / Violation Found</strong>. At least one invariant assertion failed or an Adya cycle was detected (intentionally fails CI builds).</li>\n  <li><code>2</code>: <strong>Configuration Error / Syntax Error</strong>. YAML syntax error, missing file, or initial database connection failure.</li>\n</ul>"
    },
    "trace-visualizer": {
      "id": "trace-visualizer",
      "title": "Interactive Trace Visualizer (chaossql ui)",
      "category": "Visualization",
      "summary": "Embedded HTTP visualizer with microsecond-resolution Gantt swimlanes, interactive Adya dependency graphs, and statement inspector.",
      "content": "<h2>Architecture of the Local Web Visualizer (<code>chaossql ui</code>)</h2>\n<p>The ChaosSQL Interactive Trace Visualizer is a self-contained, editorial web application served directly by Go's native HTTP server (<code>net/http</code>). Designed following the official Studio Bregalda Design System, it features an immersive, restful color palette (Deep Ink <code>#120E1F</code>, Bregalda Purple <code>#4B2E83</code>, Warm Cream <code>#FCFBF8</code>, Signal Yellow <code>#F5C400</code>, and Ship Green <code>#22C55E</code>), crisp typography (JetBrains Mono), and strictly zero external dependencies (no CDNs, no Node.js runtime, and no analytics trackers).</p>\n\n<h2>How to Launch the Visualizer</h2>\n<h3>Option 1: Immediate Post-Execution Inspection</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql run specs/banking_lost_update.yaml --ui</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>Upon completing the fuzzing run, ChaosSQL starts the local web server and automatically opens your system default browser (invoking <code>wslview</code> on WSL2, <code>open</code> on macOS, or <code>rundll32</code> on Windows).</p>\n\n<h3>Option 2: Inspecting Saved Traces from Disk</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql ui trace_banking_run_42.json --port=8090</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>The 4 Visual Inspection Modules</h2>\n\n<h3>1. Concurrency Gantt Swimlane (Microsecond Timeline)</h3>\n<p>The Gantt Swimlane maps each concurrent worker goroutine ($W_0, W_1, W_2, W_3, \\dots$) onto an independent horizontal time axis:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Monotonic Microsecond Scale:</strong> Nanosecond-precision timestamps formatted as elapsed microseconds ($\\mu\\text{s}$) from the start of the session, exposing overlapping transactions with pinpoint accuracy.</li>\n  <li><strong>Color-Coded Operation Blocks:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><span style=\"color: #6366f1;\">● Purple / Blue:</span> <code>BEGIN</code> and transaction snapshot acquisition.</li>\n      <li><span style=\"color: #94a3b8;\">● Gray:</span> <code>SELECT</code> queries and intermediate read evaluation.</li>\n      <li><span style=\"color: #f59e0b;\">● Orange:</span> <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, and mutation writes.</li>\n      <li><span style=\"color: #22c55e;\">● Green:</span> <code>COMMIT</code> successfully applied.</li>\n      <li><span style=\"color: #ef4444;\">● Red:</span> <code>ROLLBACK</code> or concurrency conflict abort.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Critical Race Window Diagnostic:</strong> Highlights the exact moment Worker $W_1$ read before Worker $W_0$ committed its write, visually pinning the lost update or dirty read.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Adya Directed Dependency Graph (Interactive SVG DSG)</h3>\n<p>Renders the Direct Serialization Graph ($SG(S)$) where vertices represent committed transactions and directed edges represent conflicting data access pairs:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Anti-Dependency Edge ($rw$):</strong> Transaction $T_1$ read a prior version of entity $x$ before transaction $T_2$ overwrote it with a newer version.</li>\n  <li><strong>Overwrite Dependency Edge ($ww$):</strong> Transaction $T_1$ wrote a version of record $x$ that was subsequently overwritten by transaction $T_2$.</li>\n  <li><strong>Read Dependency Edge ($wr$):</strong> Transaction $T_2$ read the specific value written and committed by transaction $T_1$.</li>\n  <li><strong>Anomalous Cycle Highlighting:</strong> Edges forming directed cycles ($T_1 \\xrightarrow{rw} T_2 \\xrightarrow{ww} T_1$) are illuminated in pulsing Bregalda Gold (<code>#F5C400</code>), providing mathematical visual proof of Conflict Serializability violation (CSR Theorem).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Causal Delta-Debugging ($ddmin$) Panel — Raw Trace vs Minimal Trace</h3>\n<p>ChaosSQL's shrinker module purges noise from extensive fuzzing runs to deliver a minimal reproducible trace. The $ddmin$ card compares the raw execution against the irreducible counterexample:</p>\n<table class=\"matrix-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Metric</th>\n      <th>Typical Value</th>\n      <th>Significance</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Raw Trace</td>\n      <td>20 to 100 operations</td>\n      <td>Complete execution history generated across workers during chaotic stress.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>1-Minimal Shrunk Trace</td>\n      <td>2 operations</td>\n      <td>Irreducible <em>1-minimal</em> counterexample that deterministically triggers the anomaly.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Compression Ratio</td>\n      <td>&gt; 90.0%</td>\n      <td>Volume of irrelevant non-causal operations pruned by the algorithm.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Convergence Duration</td>\n      <td>&lt; 100ms</td>\n      <td>Time spent performing causal binary search to isolate the counterexample.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>4. Tabular Query &amp; Resolved Parameters Inspector</h3>\n<p>An interactive, sortable statement ledger detailing every SQL instruction submitted to the database engine in strict chronological order:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Resolved Parameter Inspection:</strong> Statements are rendered with dynamic expression variables evaluated (e.g., <code>UPDATE accounts SET balance = 900 WHERE id = 1</code> instead of raw template placeholders).</li>\n  <li><strong>Statement Latency:</strong> Engine execution duration recorded at microsecond resolution for every roundtrip.</li>\n  <li><strong>Goroutine Worker Affinity:</strong> Immediate identification of the originating worker, thread state, and database error codes.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Standalone HTML Report Export</h2>\n<p>Generate self-contained static audit artifacts suitable for attaching to Pull Requests or sharing in architecture reviews:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql run specs/banking_lost_update.yaml --export-html=audit_report.html</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>The resulting HTML artifact is approximately 60KB, bundles all styling, interactivity, and SVG vector assets inline, and opens offline in any modern browser without network access.</p>"
    },
    "cicd-sarif": {
      "id": "cicd-sarif",
      "title": "CI/CD, SARIF 2.1.0 & GitHub Actions",
      "category": "CI/CD & Security",
      "summary": "Automated pipeline integration with GitHub Code Scanning, OASIS SARIF 2.1.0 output, JUnit XML, and OpenTelemetry OTLP tracing.",
      "content": "<h2>Continuous Concurrency Security in CI/CD</h2>\n<p>Database concurrency vulnerabilities are formally classified under <strong>CWE-362 (Concurrent Execution using Shared Resource with Improper Synchronization)</strong>. In enterprise financial and mission-critical systems, an isolation defect that permits duplicate withdrawals or inventory overselling is not merely a software bug—it represents a high-severity security and financial vulnerability.</p>\n\n<p>ChaosSQL transforms database isolation testing into an automated Continuous Quality Gate, integrating seamlessly with GitHub's native <strong>Security / Code Scanning</strong> dashboard using the international <strong>OASIS SARIF 2.1.0</strong> standard.</p>\n\n<h2>Using the Official GitHub Action (<code>action.yml</code>)</h2>\n<p>Incorporate ChaosSQL into your GitHub Actions workflow by adding <code>.github/workflows/concurrency_fuzzer.yml</code>:</p>\n\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <button class=\"copy-code-btn\" onclick=\"navigator.clipboard.writeText(document.getElementById('gh-workflow-code').innerText)\">Copy</button>\n  <pre><code id=\"gh-workflow-code\" class=\"language-yaml\">name: Database Concurrency & Isolation Audit\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [ main ]\n  pull_request:\n    branches: [ main ]\n\njobs:\n  concurrency-audit:\n    name: ChaosSQL Isolation Gating\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    permissions:\n      security-events: write # Required to publish findings to GitHub Security tab\n      contents: read\n\n    steps:\n      - name: Checkout Repository\n        uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Run ChaosSQL Action\n        uses: bregaldahq/chaossql@v1\n        with:\n          spec-path: 'specs/banking_lost_update.yaml'\n          workers: 8\n          iterations: 50\n          seed: 42\n          export-junit: 'reports/chaossql-junit.xml'\n          export-summary: '$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY'\n          export-repro: 'true'\n\n      - name: Run Audit with SARIF Export\n        if: always()\n        run: |\n          go run ./cmd/chaossql run specs/banking_lost_update.yaml \\\n            --export-sarif=reports/chaossql.sarif || true\n\n      - name: Upload SARIF to GitHub Code Scanning\n        uses: github/codeql-action/upload-sarif@v3\n        if: always()\n        with:\n          sarif_file: reports/chaossql.sarif\n          category: chaossql-isolation-audit</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>OASIS SARIF 2.1.0 Catalog: All 9 Formal Anomaly Rules</h2>\n<p>ChaosSQL's SARIF reporting engine (<code>internal/reporter/sarif.go</code>) generates structured reports adhering to <code>Static Analysis Results Interchange Format (SARIF) v2.1.0</code>, mapping each isolation anomaly to formal audit rules (<code>CHAOS001</code> through <code>CHAOS009</code>):</p>\n\n<table class=\"matrix-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Rule ID</th>\n      <th>Formal Anomaly Name</th>\n      <th>Level</th>\n      <th>Phenomenon and Recommended Remediation</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/P4-lost-update</code><br><small>(CHAOS001)</small></td>\n      <td>LostUpdateAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(239,68,68,0.2); color: #ef4444;\">error</span></td>\n      <td>Two concurrent transactions overwrite the same entity based on stale reads.<br><strong>Remediation:</strong> Upgrade to <code>SERIALIZABLE</code>, acquire pessimistic locks via <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code>, or execute atomic in-place increments.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/A5B-write-skew</code><br><small>(CHAOS002)</small></td>\n      <td>WriteSkewAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(239,68,68,0.2); color: #ef4444;\">error</span></td>\n      <td>Transactions with disjoint write sets violate a global invariant over overlapping read sets.<br><strong>Remediation:</strong> Enable Serializable Snapshot Isolation (SSI) or enforce table/materialized conflict locks.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/A5A-read-skew</code><br><small>(CHAOS003)</small></td>\n      <td>ReadSkewAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(245,196,0,0.2); color: #f5c400;\">warning</span></td>\n      <td>Transaction reads item X, concurrent transaction modifies X and Y, and first transaction reads inconsistent Y.<br><strong>Remediation:</strong> Elevate transaction isolation level to <code>REPEATABLE READ</code> or <code>SNAPSHOT ISOLATION</code>.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/G0-dirty-write</code><br><small>(CHAOS004)</small></td>\n      <td>DirtyWriteAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(239,68,68,0.2); color: #ef4444;\">error</span></td>\n      <td>Transaction modifies data previously written by another uncommitted transaction.<br><strong>Remediation:</strong> Enforce exclusive write locks throughout transaction duration (Strict 2PL).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/G1a-dirty-read</code><br><small>(CHAOS005)</small></td>\n      <td>DirtyReadAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(239,68,68,0.2); color: #ef4444;\">error</span></td>\n      <td>Transaction reads transient uncommitted mutations from another transaction that subsequently aborts.<br><strong>Remediation:</strong> Elevate isolation level to <code>READ COMMITTED</code> or higher.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/G1b-intermediate-read</code><br><small>(CHAOS006)</small></td>\n      <td>IntermediateReadAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(239,68,68,0.2); color: #ef4444;\">error</span></td>\n      <td>Transaction observes an intermediate, incomplete state of an active concurrent transaction.<br><strong>Remediation:</strong> Enforce atomic multi-version concurrency control (MVCC) visibility snapshots.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/G1c-circular-info</code><br><small>(CHAOS007)</small></td>\n      <td>CircularInfoAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(239,68,68,0.2); color: #ef4444;\">error</span></td>\n      <td>Circular read and write dependencies between concurrent transactions.<br><strong>Remediation:</strong> Upgrade to <code>SERIALIZABLE</code> to enforce an acyclic serialization graph order.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/G2-anti-dependency</code><br><small>(CHAOS008)</small></td>\n      <td>AntiDependencyCycle</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(239,68,68,0.2); color: #ef4444;\">error</span></td>\n      <td>Serialization cycle containing anti-dependency edges ($rw$), breaking strict serializability.<br><strong>Remediation:</strong> Enable true SSI (Serializable Snapshot Isolation) or predicate locking.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>chaossql/G-DL-deadlock</code><br><small>(CHAOS009)</small></td>\n      <td>DeadlockCycleAnomaly</td>\n      <td><span class=\"badge badge-cycle\" style=\"background: rgba(245,196,0,0.2); color: #f5c400;\">warning</span></td>\n      <td>Circular lock wait between concurrent transactions resulting in transactional abort.<br><strong>Remediation:</strong> Adopt a global canonical lock acquisition order (e.g., sort entities by primary key).</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>JUnit XML &amp; GitHub Step Summary Reports</h2>\n<p>Beyond SARIF, ChaosSQL generates standard CI test reports for immediate visibility across delivery pipelines:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>JUnit XML (<code>--export-junit=junit.xml</code>):</strong> Emits test suites consumable by GitHub Actions Test Reporter, GitLab CI, and Azure Pipelines. Concurrency failures are mapped to <code>&lt;failure&gt;</code> tags containing anomaly classifications and embedded Mermaid sequence graphs.</li>\n  <li><strong>GitHub Step Summary (<code>--export-summary=$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY</code>):</strong> Renders rich GitHub-flavored Markdown tables summarizing execution metrics, caught anomalies, and the minimal reproducible counterexample directly on the GitHub Actions run summary page.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Observability with OpenTelemetry (OTLP JSON)</h2>\n<p>Supplying the <code>--export-otel=trace_otel.json</code> flag converts the entire concurrent execution schedule into a distributed trace conforming to the OpenTelemetry Tracing API. Each worker goroutine is represented as a root Span, and each SQL query is recorded as a child Span enriched with semantic conventions (<code>db.system</code>, <code>db.statement</code>, <code>db.operation</code>, <code>thread.id</code>). The exported JSON file can be ingested directly into Jaeger, Grafana Tempo, or Datadog for deep concurrency and latency profiling.</p>"
    },
    "drivers": {
      "id": "drivers",
      "title": "Supported Database Engines & Drivers",
      "category": "Engines",
      "summary": "Deep dive into pure-Go SQLite, PostgreSQL pgx connection pooling, and MySQL/MariaDB isolation quirks and gap locks.",
      "content": "<h2>Driver Architecture and the <code>DatabaseDriver</code> Interface</h2>\n<p>ChaosSQL interacts with relational database engines through a high-performance abstract interface defined in <code>internal/drivers/driver.go</code>:</p>\n\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-go\">type DatabaseDriver interface {\n    DriverName() string\n    Open(ctx context.Context) error\n    Close() error\n    Reset(ctx context.Context, schemaSQL, seedSQL string) error\n    BeginTx(ctx context.Context) (Tx, error)\n    QueryRow(ctx context.Context, query string, args ...any) *sql.Row\n    Query(ctx context.Context, query string, args ...any) (*sql.Rows, error)\n    Exec(ctx context.Context, query string, args ...any) (sql.Result, error)\n}</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>The <code>Reset()</code> method is pivotal for deterministic fuzzing: prior to each test iteration and before every step of the Delta-Debugging ($ddmin$) algorithm, ChaosSQL re-initializes the database state from scratch, guaranteeing pristine isolation between execution runs.</p>\n\n<h2>Pure-Go SQLite Driver (<code>modernc.org/sqlite</code>)</h2>\n\n<h3>Compiling Without CGO (<code>CGO_ENABLED=0</code>)</h3>\n<p>Unlike legacy Go drivers based on <code>mattn/go-sqlite3</code> that require external C compilers, libc headers, and shared dynamic libraries, ChaosSQL utilizes <code>modernc.org/sqlite</code>. This enables compiling universal static binaries with zero external build dependencies.</p>\n\n<h3>WAL Configuration and High-Concurrency Tuning</h3>\n<p>During driver initialization in <code>Open()</code>, the SQLite driver automatically enforces optimized concurrency PRAGMAs:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- Enable Write-Ahead Logging to permit concurrent readers alongside an active writer\nPRAGMA journal_mode = WAL;\n\n-- Set lock acquisition busy timeout before failing (5,000 milliseconds)\nPRAGMA busy_timeout = 5000;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Supported Connection Strings (DSN)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>:memory:</code> or <code>file:chaos_mem?mode=memory&cache=shared</code>: In-memory volatile database executing in under 50ms (ideal for lightning-fast CI test suites, benchmarked at over 13.9M ops/s).</li>\n  <li><code>file:database.db</code>: Persistent disk-backed database for local stress testing and crash recovery audits.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>PostgreSQL Driver (<code>pgx/v5</code>)</h2>\n<p>ChaosSQL's PostgreSQL driver is built upon <code>github.com/jackc/pgx/v5/stdlib</code>, the gold standard for high-performance PostgreSQL drivers in Go.</p>\n\n<h3>Connection String (DSN)</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>postgres://chaos_user:secret_pass@localhost:5432/chaostest?sslmode=disable</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Clean State Reset via Drop Cascade</h3>\n<p>To ensure a pristine database state prior to every execution, the PostgreSQL driver executes:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code class=\"language-sql\">DROP SCHEMA IF EXISTS public CASCADE;\nCREATE SCHEMA public;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Isolation Semantics: READ COMMITTED vs REPEATABLE READ vs SERIALIZABLE</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong><code>READ COMMITTED</code> (PostgreSQL Default):</strong> Each individual statement sees a new snapshot taken at the start of that specific statement (<em>statement-level snapshot</em>). Highly vulnerable to <strong>Lost Update ($P4$)</strong> and <strong>Read Skew ($A5A$)</strong> when applications perform read-modify-write patterns.</li>\n  <li><strong><code>REPEATABLE READ</code>:</strong> Implements Snapshot Isolation (SI). The entire transaction observes a consistent snapshot captured at the start of the first statement. Prevents $P4$ and $A5A$, but remains vulnerable to <strong>Write Skew ($A5B$)</strong> because transactions with disjoint write sets bypass write-conflict locks.</li>\n  <li><strong><code>SERIALIZABLE</code>:</strong> Activates Ports &amp; Grittner's <strong>Serializable Snapshot Isolation (SSI)</strong> algorithm (VLDB 2012). The engine monitors virtual read locks (<code>SIREAD</code>) and automatically aborts transactions participating in anti-dependency cycles with SQLSTATE <code>40001 (serialization_failure)</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Deadlock Handling in PostgreSQL</h3>\n<p>The driver catches SQLSTATE <code>40P01 (deadlock_detected)</code> and seamlessly normalizes it into the canonical error type <code>ErrDeadlockDetected</code>, categorized by ChaosSQL under anomaly <code>G-DL</code>.</p>\n\n<h2>MySQL &amp; MariaDB Driver (<code>go-sql-driver/mysql</code>)</h2>\n<p>The MySQL driver leverages <code>github.com/go-sql-driver/mysql</code> configured with multiple statement support (<code>multiStatements=true</code>).</p>\n\n<h3>Connection String (DSN)</h3>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>root:password@tcp(127.0.0.1:3306)/chaostest?parseTime=true&multiStatements=true</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>InnoDB Storage Engine Particulars</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Default <code>REPEATABLE READ</code>:</strong> Unlike PostgreSQL, InnoDB defaults to <code>REPEATABLE READ</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Next-Key Locks and Gap Locks:</strong> InnoDB locks not only matched index records but also the open index intervals (<em>gaps</em>) between them, preventing phantom insertions ($A3$).</li>\n  <li><strong>Pessimistic Locking Deadlocks:</strong> Concurrent transactions running <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> on secondary indexes are prone to gap deadlocks. ChaosSQL intercepts MySQL error <code>1213 (ER_LOCK_DEADLOCK)</code> and maps it to the anomaly catalog.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Cross-Engine Differential Fuzzing with <code>chaossql diff</code></h2>\n<p>The <code>diff</code> subcommand executes identical workloads with identical PRNG seeds across two distinct database engines simultaneously, highlighting semantic discrepancies in transactional behavior:</p>\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <pre><code>chaossql diff specs/banking_lost_update.yaml \\\n  --driver-a=sqlite --dsn-a=\":memory:\" \\\n  --driver-b=postgres --dsn-b=\"postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/chaostest\" \\\n  --seed=42</code></pre>\n</div>"
    },
    "go-sdk": {
      "id": "go-sdk",
      "title": "Go Developer Testing SDK (chaostest)",
      "category": "SDK",
      "summary": "Programmatic API in pkg/chaostest for embedding deterministic isolation fuzzing directly into standard go test suites.",
      "content": "<h2>Introduction to the SDK (<code>pkg/chaostest</code>)</h2>\n<p>The <strong>ChaosSQL Go Developer Testing SDK</strong> (<code>pkg/chaostest</code>) is built for software engineers who want to embed deterministic concurrency and invariant fuzzing directly into standard Go test suites (<code>*testing.T</code>). It allows developers to validate database repositories, persistence layers, and domain services natively via <code>go test ./...</code>, offering full compatibility with <code>go test -race</code> without requiring external binaries or CLI orchestration.</p>\n\n<h2>Fluent Builder API: <code>chaostest.New(t)</code> &amp; <code>NewHarness(t)</code></h2>\n<p>The package provides a fluent builder pattern (<code>chaostest.New(t)</code> or <code>chaostest.NewHarness(t)</code>) with method chaining to configure concurrency test harnesses intuitively:</p>\n\n<table class=\"matrix-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Method</th>\n      <th>Parameters</th>\n      <th>Technical Description</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>New(t testing.TB)</code><br><small>or <code>NewHarness(t)</code></small></td>\n      <td><code>testing.TB</code></td>\n      <td>Initializes a new test harness bound to the lifecycle of the current testing context.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>WithDriver(driver)</code></td>\n      <td><code>drivers.DatabaseDriver</code></td>\n      <td>Sets the database driver instance. If omitted, automatically provisions an isolated in-memory SQLite database.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>WithSchema(sql)</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Defines DDL SQL statements for table and index creation.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>WithSeed(sql)</code></td>\n      <td><code>string</code></td>\n      <td>Defines DML SQL statements for initial dataset seeding prior to fuzzing.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>WithInvariant(name, query, expr)</code></td>\n      <td><code>string, string, string</code></td>\n      <td>Registers a global consistency rule that must hold true upon completion.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>WithJitter(minMs, maxMs)</code></td>\n      <td><code>int, int</code></td>\n      <td>Configures stochastic microsecond jitter injected between transactional steps (default: 1ms to 5ms).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>AddOperation(name, steps...)</code></td>\n      <td><code>string, ...string</code></td>\n      <td>Registers a concurrent transaction. Supports variable capture (<code>-&gt; var</code>) and expressions (<code>{var - 100}</code>).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>AddOperationWithParams(name, params, steps...)</code></td>\n      <td><code>string, map[string]string, ...string</code></td>\n      <td>Registers an operation with dynamically randomized per-transaction parameters.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>Test Assertions: <code>AssertNoAnomalies</code> vs <code>AssertAnomalyDetected</code></h2>\n\n<h3>1. <code>AssertNoAnomalies(ctx, workers, iterations, seed)</code></h3>\n<p>Positive assertion: verifies that the service or repository implementation is 100% resilient against concurrency anomalies under the configured schedule. If an invariant is violated or an anomaly is detected, the method invokes <code>t.Fatalf</code>, logging:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Violated invariant name, expected condition, and actual query result.</li>\n  <li>Formal Adya anomaly classification (e.g., <code>P4_LOST_UPDATE</code>).</li>\n  <li>Causal Delta-Debugging ($ddmin$) compression statistics: shrinking $N$ operations down to the minimal counterexample.</li>\n  <li>Copy-pasteable Mermaid sequence diagram illustrating the conflicting schedule.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. <code>AssertAnomalyDetected(ctx, workers, iterations, seed, expectedAnomaly...)</code></h3>\n<p>Negative assertion: used in regression test suites to empirically prove that unmitigated code or insufficient transaction isolation levels trigger the expected anomaly. Guarantees that regression suites are free of false negatives.</p>\n\n<h2>Complete Example: Testing and Mitigating Lost Update</h2>\n<p>The example below demonstrates how to write a native Go unit test proving the effectiveness of an atomic update mitigation against Lost Update ($P4$):</p>\n\n<div class=\"code-container\">\n  <button class=\"copy-code-btn\" onclick=\"navigator.clipboard.writeText(document.getElementById('go-sdk-code').innerText)\">Copy</button>\n  <pre><code id=\"go-sdk-code\" class=\"language-go\">package bank_test\n\nimport (\n    \"context\"\n    \"testing\"\n\n    \"github.com/bregaldahq/chaossql/internal/drivers\"\n    \"github.com/bregaldahq/chaossql/pkg/chaostest\"\n)\n\n// TestAccountWithdrawal_AtomicMitigation verifies that concurrent withdrawals\n// utilizing an atomic update prevent the Lost Update anomaly (P4).\nfunc TestAccountWithdrawal_AtomicMitigation(t *testing.T) {\n    ctx := context.Background()\n\n    // 1. Instantiate database driver (pure-Go in-memory SQLite)\n    driver := drivers.NewSQLiteDriver(\"file:bank_test?mode=memory&cache=shared\")\n    defer driver.Close()\n\n    // 2. Build concurrency fuzzer harness using the fluent SDK\n    tester := chaostest.NewHarness(t).\n        WithDriver(driver).\n        WithSchema(`\n            CREATE TABLE accounts (\n                id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n                balance INT NOT NULL\n            );\n            CREATE TABLE ledger (\n                id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n                amount INT NOT NULL\n            );\n        `).\n        WithSeed(\"INSERT INTO accounts (id, balance) VALUES (1, 1000);\").\n        WithInvariant(\n            \"ledger_balance_consistency\",\n            \"SELECT (SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1) AS actual, \" +\n            \"(1000 - COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0)) AS expected FROM ledger;\",\n            \"actual == expected\",\n        ).\n        WithJitter(1, 4).\n        // Mitigated transaction: atomic UPDATE with balance guard predicate\n        AddOperation(\"atomic_withdrawal\",\n            \"UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1 AND balance >= 100;\",\n            \"INSERT INTO ledger (amount) VALUES (100);\",\n        )\n\n    // 3. Execute fuzzer across 8 concurrent workers and 40 transactions\n    // Must pass with 100% success and zero invariant violations\n    tester.AssertNoAnomalies(ctx, 8, 40, 42)\n}</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Automated Synthesis of <code>repro_test.go</code> via $ddmin$</h2>\n<p>When a test executed via the SDK fails under <code>AssertNoAnomalies</code>, the Causal Delta-Debugging ($ddmin$) engine isolates the 2-operation minimal counterexample and synthesizes ready-to-run Go source code for an independent <code>repro_test.go</code> directly into the test logs.</p>\n<p>Engineers can copy this generated test into a local file and attach Delve debugger breakpoints, reproducing the race condition in 5 milliseconds with zero fuzzer overhead.</p>\n\n<div class=\"docs-callout tip\">\n  <strong>Strict Determinism in CI:</strong> When configuring regression tests in Go, always supply a constant static seed (e.g., <code>seed: 42</code>). This guarantees lightning-fast, reproducible, and non-flaky test execution across CI runners.\n</div>"
    },
    "academic-theory": {
      "id": "academic-theory",
      "title": "Formal Theory & Mathematical Foundation",
      "category": "Theory",
      "summary": "Mathematical foundations of concurrency fuzzing: Bernstein conditions, Adya CSR theorems, Burckhardt PCT probability bounds, and Zeller delta-debugging.",
      "content": "<h2>1. Formal Model of Transactions and Execution Schedules</h2>\n<p>ChaosSQL establishes its mathematical foundations upon classical database concurrency theory developed by Bernstein, Goodman, Papadimitriou, and Adya.</p>\n\n<h3>Formal Definition of a Transaction</h3>\n<p>A transaction $T_i$ is formally defined as an ordered tuple:</p>\n<p>$$T_i = (O_i, <_i)$$</p>\n<p>Where:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>$O_i$ is a finite set of atomic read operations $r_i[x]$, write operations $w_i[x]$, commit $c_i$, or abort $a_i$, accessing data items $x \\in \\mathcal{D}$.</li>\n  <li>$<_i$ is a strict partial order over $O_i$ reflecting the deterministic program flow of the transaction.</li>\n  <li>There exists exactly one terminal operation $t_i \\in \\{a_i, c_i\\}$, which is the maximal element under $<_i$.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Formal Definition of an Execution Schedule (History)</h3>\n<p>A concurrent execution schedule $S$ over a finite set of transactions $\\mathcal{T} = \\{T_1, T_2, \\dots, T_n\\}$ is a partial order $S = (\\bigcup_{i=1}^n O_i, <_S)$ satisfying:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>$\\bigcup_{i=1}^n <_i \\;\\subseteq\\; <_S$ (preserves the intra-transaction instruction ordering of each transaction).</li>\n  <li>For any two conflicting operations $o_i, o_j \\in S$ (where $i \\neq j$), either $o_i <_S o_j$ or $o_j <_S o_i$.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>2. Bernstein Conflict Conditions &amp; CSR Theorem</h2>\n\n<h3>Bernstein Conflict Condition (1966)</h3>\n<p>Two operations $o_i, o_j \\in S$ belonging to distinct transactions ($i \\neq j$) are in <strong>direct conflict</strong> if and only if they access the same data entity $x \\in \\mathcal{D}$ and at least one of them is a write mutation ($w$):</p>\n<p>$$\\text{Conflict}(o_i, o_j) \\iff (\\text{target}(o_i) = \\text{target}(o_j) = x) \\;\\land\\; (o_i \\text{ is } w \\;\\lor\\; o_j \\text{ is } w)$$</p>\n\n<h3>Direct Serialization Graph (Adya Dependency Graph)</h3>\n<p>Given an execution schedule $S$, the Direct Serialization Graph $SG(S) = (V, E)$ is a directed graph where:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Vertices $V = \\mathcal{T}$ correspond to the set of committed transactions.</li>\n  <li>A directed edge $(T_i \\to T_j) \\in E$ exists if and only if there exist $o_i \\in T_i$ and $o_j \\in T_j$ such that $o_i <_S o_j$ and $\\text{Conflict}(o_i, o_j)$.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>The Three Fundamental Dependency Relations (Atul Adya, 1999)</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Read Dependency ($wr$):</strong> $T_i \\xrightarrow{wr} T_j$ if $T_i$ writes a version of $x$ and $T_j$ subsequently reads that same version.</li>\n  <li><strong>Overwrite Dependency ($ww$):</strong> $T_i \\xrightarrow{ww} T_j$ if $T_i$ writes a version of $x$ and $T_j$ subsequently overwrites $x$.</li>\n  <li><strong>Anti-Dependency ($rw$):</strong> $T_i \\xrightarrow{rw} T_j$ if $T_i$ reads a version of $x$ and $T_j$ subsequently writes a newer version of $x$, invalidating the currency of $T_i$'s read.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Fundamental Theorem of Conflict Serializability (CSR)</h3>\n<div class=\"docs-callout info\">\n  <strong>Fundamental Theorem:</strong> A concurrent history $S$ is Conflict Serializable ($S \\in \\text{CSR}$) if and only if its Serialization Graph $SG(S)$ is a <strong>Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG)</strong>.\n</div>\n<p>The existence of any directed cycle ($T_1 \\to T_2 \\to \\dots \\to T_1$) proves that the execution is semantically inconsistent and possesses no equivalent serial ordering.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Probabilistic Concurrency Testing (PCT-SQL) (Burckhardt &amp; Musuvathi)</h2>\n<p>Naive random concurrency testing fails due to combinatorial explosion. For $n$ transactions executing $m$ steps each, the total number of possible interleavings $\\Omega$ grows factorially:</p>\n<p>$$|\\Omega| = \\frac{(n \\cdot m)!}{(m!)^n}$$</p>\n<p>For merely 5 transactions with 3 steps each, there exist <strong>168,168,000 possible interleavings</strong>. The probability of hitting a critical context switch purely by chance is below $10^{-6}$.</p>\n\n<h3>Burckhardt Lower Bound Theorem (ASPLOS 2010)</h3>\n<p>ChaosSQL adopts the <em>Probabilistic Concurrency Testing</em> (PCT) algorithm. Burckhardt and Musuvathi proved that if a concurrency anomaly has a schedule depth $d$ (the number of priority constraints or critical context switches required to trigger it), the algorithm guarantees a rigorous lower bound on detection probability in a single run:</p>\n<p>$$\\mathbb{P}(\\text{Detection}) \\ge \\frac{1}{n \\cdot k^{d-1}}$$</p>\n<p>Where $n$ is the number of concurrent threads and $k$ is the total number of executed steps.</p>\n\n<h3>Practical Application to Relational Databases</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Seminal empirical research (Lu et al., ASPLOS) demonstrates that <strong>~96% of real-world concurrency bugs have depth $d = 1$ or $d = 2$</strong> (Lost Update requires $d=1$; Write Skew requires $d=2$).</li>\n  <li>For $d = 2$, the single-run detection probability is $\\mathbb{P} \\ge \\frac{1}{n \\cdot k}$. In a test with 4 workers and 25 steps ($n=4, k=25$), $\\mathbb{P} \\ge \\frac{1}{100} = 1\\%$.</li>\n  <li>In a short batch of only $R = 300$ iterations (which completes in &lt; 1 second in-memory):</li>\n</ul>\n<p>$$\\mathbb{P}(\\text{Find Bug in } R \\text{ Runs}) = 1 - \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{100}\\right)^{300} \\approx 1 - e^{-3} \\approx \\mathbf{95.02\\%}$$</p>\n\n<h2>4. Causal Minimization with Andreas Zeller Delta-Debugging ($ddmin$)</h2>\n\n<h3>Formal Definition of $1$-Minimality</h3>\n<p>Let $C = \\langle op_1, op_2, \\dots, op_N \\rangle$ be the ordered sequence of scheduled operations that triggers an invariant failure $\\text{test}(C) = \\text{FAIL}$.</p>\n<p>A subsequence $C^* \\subseteq C$ is defined as <strong>$1$-Minimal</strong> if and only if:</p>\n<p>$$\\text{test}(C^*) = \\text{FAIL} \\quad \\land \\quad \\forall op \\in C^*: \\text{test}(C^* \\setminus \\{op\\}) = \\text{PASS}$$</p>\n\n<h3>The Causal-$ddmin$ Algorithm in ChaosSQL</h3>\n<p>In relational databases, standard binary partitioning of classical $ddmin$ fails because deleting an operation that generated a foreign key parent record produces artificial <code>FOREIGN KEY constraint failed</code> errors.</p>\n<p>ChaosSQL solves this via <strong>Causal Delta-Debugging</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Prior to testing a candidate subset $C' \\subset C$, the engine computes the Data Causality Graph.</li>\n  <li>It calculates the causal closure $\\text{Closure}(C')$: if $T_j \\in C'$ references a key created by $T_i$, $T_i$ is mandatorily preserved in the test slice.</li>\n  <li>This eliminates 100% of false positives caused by broken referential integrity and accelerates convergence by up to <strong>$4\\times$</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>5. Linearizability and Black-Box Observations (Elle)</h2>\n<p>Inspired by the testing methodology of Kyle Kingsbury (Jepsen) and Peter Alvaro (VLDB 2020), ChaosSQL infers transactional anomalies strictly from client-observable operation histories (reads, writes, commits, and returns). No internal database engine instrumentation or custom storage bytecode is required, making ChaosSQL universally compatible with any SQL-compliant relational database.</p>"
    }
  }
};

